1300:
** διατελ-έω**,
_future_ -τελέσω, _Attic dialect_ -τελῶ: _perfect_ διατετέλεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.4, “IG” 22.223 A 5}}:—**bring quite to an end, accomplish,** ἐπεί περ ἠρξάμην, διατελέσαι βούλομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.4; δ. χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 434}}; so of Time, διατετελεκὼς τὰ ἐν τοῖς ἐφήβοις δέκα ἔτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** _absolutely_,
**II.1** mostly with _participle_, **continue being** or **doing** so and so, τὸ λοιπὸν τῆς ζόης δ. ἐόντα τυφλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.117; δ. ἐόντες ἐλεύθεροι [prev. author] 7.111, compare 1.32, etc.; δ. τὸν λοιπὸν βίον δουλεύοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38; δ. καθεύδοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a; μινυρίζων δ. τὸν βίον ὅλον [prev. author] “R.” 411a}}; διετέλεσας πειρώμενος you **have been** trying **all along,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 206a: with _adjectives_, δ. πρόθυμος}} **continue** zealous, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89, compare 1.34; δ. ἀχίτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.2; ἡδὺς δ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 45.9.}}
**II.2** with no _participle_ or _adjective_, **continue, live,** δ. μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων διὰ βίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192c; δ. χαριέντως [prev. author] “R.” 426a; ἐν ἀγρῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 4.}}
**II.2.b** generally, **continue, persevere,** διατέλει ὥσπερ ἤρξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494c; δ. ἐν ὕπνῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 779a24; ἐν τῇ θαλάττῃ [prev. author] “Pr.” 933a14}}; of things, **continue,** ἐὰν αἱ μιμήσεις ἐκ νέων πόρρω δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395d.}}

1301:
** διατηρ-έω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ **δια-τᾱρέω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 1.132.8 (2nd c.BC)}}, but _Doric dialect_ **δια-τηρέω** {{Refs|“SIG” 541 A 4}}:— **watch closely, observe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836d (variant), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 612b28; δ. μή τι πάθωσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.20.}}
**2** **maintain,** τὴν ἐλευθερίαν Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.184; τὴν τάξιν Decrees [prev. work]37; τὸ πρέπον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1178a13; τὰ τοῦ βίου δίκαια 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 637; τὴν πόλιν καὶ τὴν ἑαυτῶν πίστιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.7; τὴν εὔνοιαν “IG” 12(7).506; τὴν ἀφθαρσίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 19, etc.:}} —_middle_, -εῖται τὸν καιρόν **observes,**{{Refs|[prev. work] “fragment” 77}}:—_passive_, ὅταν διατηρηθῶσιν οἱ νόμοι τῇ πόλει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.6; ἀλειτούργητος -τηρείσθω ἡ θεία φύσις 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.42U.}}
**3** with predicates, βοῦς ἐννέα ἔτη δ. ἀνοχεύτους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 595b18; ἀβλαβές δ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.8.7; ἀφλυκταίνωτα δ. τὰ μέρη 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.156; δ. τὸν πόλεμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 33.}}
**4** δ. ἑαυτὸν ἔκ τινος **keep** oneself from.., {{NT|NT.Act.15.29.}}

1303:
** διατίθημι**,
_3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperfect_ διετίθουν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antipho Tragicus 1}}:—**arrange** each **in their several places, distribute,** τὰ κρέα, in sacrificing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132; τὸ μὲν ἐπὶ δεξιά, τὸ δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερά [prev. author] 7.39; ᾗπερ οἱ θεοὶ διέθεσαν τὰ ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27; δ. οἶνον εἰς ὀστράκια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 594a11.}}
**II** **manage** well or ill, usually with _adverb_, κράτιστα δ. τὰ τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.15; καλὸν πρᾶγμα κακῶς δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.88}}; of persons, δ. ἑωυτὸν ἀνηκέστως **treat** himself barbarously, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155:}} —_passive_, οὐ ῥᾳδίως διετέθη he **was** not very gently **handled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.57}}; ἀπόρως διατεθέντας **reduced to** helplessness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.23; ἀθλίως διατιθέμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 121b; σῶμα διατεθειμένῳ κακῶς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 591.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _person_, with _adverbs_, **dispose** one so or so, ὅταν οὕτω διαθῇς τοὺς Ἕλληνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.80; οὕτω διαθεὶς.. τὰς πόλεις πρὸς ἀλλήλας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.168; δ. τινὰς ἀπίστως πρὸς ἡμᾶς αὐτούς [prev. author] 20.22; τὸν ἀκροατὴν δ. πως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1356a3}}:—_passive_, **to be disposed** in a certain manner, πρός τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151c, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.14; οἰκειότερον διατεθῆναί τινι [prev. author] 12.160; τὸν εἰρημένον τρόπον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302a35}}; ἐρωτικῶς δ., of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207c, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.15 (διάκειμαι is more usually as _passive_ in this sense).}}
**III** **set forth,** of speakers, minstrels, etc., **recite,** κακῶς ποιήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 162d, compare “Lg.” 658d; compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6.}}
**III.2** **describe,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.16, etc.}}
**B** _middle_, **arrange as one likes, dispose of,** τὴν θυγατέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.7; τὰ σώματα ἐπονειδίστως δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.140; οὔθ᾽ ὅσ᾽ ἂν πορίσωσι.. ταῦτ᾽ ἔχοντες διαθέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16; εἰς καλὸν δ. τὰ πεπραγμένα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 51, compare “Merc.Cond.” 25}}; **spend,** δ. τὰς οὐσίας εἴς τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.6.5: _metaphorically_, τὸ πλεῖον τῆς ὀργῆς εἴς τινα [prev. author] 16.1.2.}}
**B.2** **dispose of one's** property, **devise** it **by will,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.68; τὴν οὐσίαν ἑτέρῳ [prev. author] 7.1}}; δ. διαθήκας, διαθήκην, **make** a will, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 922c: _absolutely_, [prev. passage], 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.41}}; κἂν ἀποθάνῃ μὴ διαθέμενος **intestate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270a28}}; ὁ δ. **the testator,** {{NT|NT.Heb.9.16.}}
**B.3** **dispose of** merchandise, φόρτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, 194, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.10, “Ath.” 2.11; τισί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 849d; ἔλαιον καὶ κίκι “PRev.Laws” 48.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; δ. τὴν ὥραν καὶ τὴν σοφίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.13; δ. τι τριπλασίας τιμῆς ἢ πρότερον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.31.}}
**B.4** **arrange** or **settle mutually,** δ. διαθήκην τινί **make a covenant with** one, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 439; δ. διαθήκην πρός τινα NT.Act.3.25}}; ἔριν δ. ἀλλήλοις **settle** a quarrel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.23; ὡμολόγνσαν καὶ διέθεντο ὀφείλειν “IG” 12(7).67.58 (from Amorgos).}}
**B.5** **compose, make,** νόμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834a.}}
**B.6** **set forth, recite,** λόγους, δημηγορίαν, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.108.2, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.7, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.17; πολλοὺς ἐπαίνους τινῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.17; δ. ῥῆσιν ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 1.}}
**B.6.b** Grammars, διατιθέναι and -τίθεσθαι **to act** and **be acted upon,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 12.15}}; τὸ διατιθέν and τὸ διατιθέμενον **subject** and **object,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 127.22.}}

1304:
** διατρίβω** [ρῑ],
_perfect_ **-τέτρῐφα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.57.3:}} —_passive_, _aorist 2_ διετρίβην [ῐ] (see. below):—**rub hard,** χερσὶ διατρίψας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.847}}: more frequently, **wear away, consume,** πάντα διατρίβουσιν Ἁχαιοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.265; χρήματα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 921; τὰ τῶν Πελοποννησίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87}}; εἰς αἰτίας ἀλόγους δ. τὸ θεῖον **to fritter away** Providence into unreasoning causes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 23:}} — _passive_, κάκιστα διατριβῆναι **perish** utterly, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.120 (variant{ἐκ-}),compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.78.}}]
**II** **spend,** of Time, θερείην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189; frequently χρόνον δ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11; παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, etc.; δ. τινὰς ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.49; ἓξ ἔτη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.141 (later with _genitive_, ἐτῶν οὐκ ὀλίγων ἐν Ῥώμῃ δ. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.10.2)}}:— _passive_, ἐνιαυτὸς διετρίβη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.125.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_ (without χρόνον), **waste time,** οὐ μὴ διατρίψεις..; **make** no more **delay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 462}}; δ. ἐν γυμνασίοις **pass all one's time** there, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1002; ἐν ἄστει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.14; ἐν ἀγρῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 71.6}}; αὐτοῦ, ἔνδον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a; δ. μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων}}**go on talking,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 59d, etc.}}: hence, **busy, employ oneself,** ἐν ζητήσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 29c; ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ [prev. author] “Tht.” 173c; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Euthd.” 305a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16; ἀμφί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 2.1; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90c, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.4; πρὸς ἱππικῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 126c; πρὸς τοῖς ἔργοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a8}}; πρὸς φιλοσοφίᾳ (probable reading for -ίαν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540b}}: with _participle_, δ. μελετῶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12.}}
**II.2.b** _absolutely_, **lose time, delay,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.150, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 19, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 515, etc.; λέγε καὶ μὴ διάτριβε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472b}}; διατέτρῐφα I **have let the time slip by..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 143a: with}}_participle_, καθ᾽ ἕκαστα λέγων δ. **to waste time** in speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.35, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.9.}}
**II.3** **reside,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHal. 1.182 (3rd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 22.6 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**III** **put off by delay, thwart, hinder,** μή τι διατρίβειν ἐμὸν χόλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.42; οὔ τι διατρίβω μητρὸς γάμον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.341; τἄριστον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 503:}} with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ὄφρα κεν ἥ γε διατρίβῃσιν Ἀχαιοὺς ὃν γάμον **put** them **off** in the matter of her wedding, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.204:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, μὴ δηθὰ διατρίβωμεν ὁδοῖο **let us** not **lose time** on the way, {{Refs|[prev. work] 404}}:—_middle_, μή τι διατριβώμεθα πείρης {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.883.}}

1306:
** διαυγ-άζω**,
**glance, shine through,** τῷ σχισμῷ {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.3.3; ἕως οὗ ἡμέρα διαυγάσῃ NT.2Pet.1.19: _impersonal_, ἅμα τῷ διαυγάζειν (i.e. τὴν ἡμέραν) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.104.5}}; **to be transparent,** Mnesith. cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus (?) 15.11.}}
**II** ={φωτίζω}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: and so _metaphorically_, διαυγασθείς **being enlightened, perceiving the truth,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.10.4.}}
**III** Astrology texts, **influence by its rays** (= ἐπιθεωρέω), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.70 (1st c.AD).}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be glazed,** of pottery, probably in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1143.15 (1st c.BC).}}

1307:
** διαφαν-ής**, ές,
(φαίνω) **translucent, transparent,** [ὕαλος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 767; οὖρα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.72, “Epid.” 1.26. ; ὦτα [prev. author] “Coac.” 188; ὑδάτια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229b; χιτώνια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 48; χιτωνάριον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 727, compare “IG” 5(1).1390.16,21; τὸ δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 418b4, +others}}
**2** **red-hot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.9, 4.73, 75, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **manifest,** τάδ᾽ ἤδη διαφανῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 754}}; **distinct, distinctly seen,** φλέβες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.3.17; εἶδος δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544c, 548c (_superlative_)}}. _adverb_ **-νῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.24}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.46.}}
**II.2** **conspicuous,** ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600b; εἰς ἅπαντας ἀνθρώπους ἀρετῇ [prev. author] “Ti.” 25b.}}
**III** _substantive_ δ., τό, **talc,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.663, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 99.}}

1308:
** διαφέρω**,
_future_ διοίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 321, διοίσομαι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 255, etc.:}}_aorist 1_ διήνεγκα, _Ionic dialect_ διήνεικα: _aorist 2_ διήνεγκον:—**carry over** or **across,** δ. ναῦς τὸν Ἰσθμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8}}; **carry from one to another,** διαφέρεις κηρύγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 382; [τὸ ἤλεκτρον] διαφέρεται εἰς τοὺς Ἕλληνας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 836b6: _metaphorically_, γλῶσσαν διοίσει}} **will put** the tongue **in motion,** will speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 323 codices}}
**2** of Time, δ. τὸν αἰῶνα, τὸν βίον, **go through** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” [10]; νύκτα [prev. author] “Rh.” 600: _absolutely_, ἄπαις διοίσει [prev. work] 982}}:—_middle_, **live, continue,** ὑγιηροὶ τἄλλα διαφέρονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 56}}; σοῦ διοίσεται μόνος **will pass his life** apart from thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 511; σκοπούμενος διοίσει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.24 (conjecture Dind. for διέσῃ).}}
**3** **bear through, bear to the end,** σκῆπτρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1195}}; γαστρὸς ὄγκον δ., of a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5: hence}}
**4** **bear to the end, go through with,** πόλεμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.25, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.11}}; but also, **bear the burden of** war, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.54}}; **endure, support,** with an _adverb_, ῥᾷστα γὰρ τὸ σόν τε σὺ κἀγὼ διοίσω τοὐμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 321; δ. πότμον δάκρυσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1143 (Lyric poetry)}}: _absolutely_, of patients in disease, δ. ἕως τῶν εἰκοσιτεσσάρων ἡμερέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 40; δ. φθειρόμενος [prev. work] 12 (also ἡ νοῦσος δ. ἐννέα ἔτεα [prev. passage]).}}
**II** **carry different ways,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 570, etc.; δ. ἕκαστα εἰς τὰς χώρας τὰς προσηκούσας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.8}}; **toss about,** ὅπλισμα.. διαφέρων ἐσφενδόνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 715}}; δ. τὰς κόρας **to turn** the eyes **about,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1087. “Or.” 1261 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, **to be drawn apart, disrupted,** opposed to συμφέρεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 908.2}}; **to be tossed about,** uncertain in {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.5; δ. ἐν τῷ Ἀδρίᾳ NT.Act.27.27, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 26.}}
**II.2** δ. τινά **spread** his **fame abroad,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.60; εἰς ἅπαντας τὴν ἐκείνου μνήμην δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.46}}:—_passive_, φήμη διηνέχθη {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.163c.}}
**II.3** **tear asunder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 754}}; **disjoin,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a34 (_passive_)}}: _metaphorically_, **distract,** τὰς ψυχὰς φροντίσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.133d, compare 97f (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 32.46 (_passive_).}}
**II.4** δ. τὴν ψῆφον **give** one's vote **a different way,** i.e. against another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.138, etc.}}; but also, **give each man** his vote, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 49, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.8.}}
**II.5** ἐράνους δ., ={διαλύεσθαι}, **pay** them **up, discharge** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 22.}}
**II.6** **defer, reserve** for judgement, τὸν αἴτιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 68 (Lyric poetry, διασπαράσσει Scholia).}}
**II.7** **plunder,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.90}}:—_passive_, τῶν ἀπὸ [τῆς οἰκίας] φορτίων διενηνεγμένων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PLond.” 1.45.9 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.8** **excel,** ἀρετῇ τοὺς ἄλλους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.67, compare 2.5; καλλιτεκνίᾳ πάσας γυναῖκας “Stud.Pont.” 3.123 (from Amasia).}}
**III** _intransitive_, **differ,** φυᾷ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.54}}; ἆρ᾽ οἱ τεκόντες διαφέρουσιν ἢ τροφα; is it one's parents or nurture **that make the difference?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 599}}: with _genitive_, **to be different from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 251, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.86, etc.; οὐδὲν διοίσεις Χαιρεφῶντος τὴν φύσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 503, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329d; τὸ δ᾽.. ἀφανίζειν ἱερὰ ἔσθ᾽ ὅτι τοῦ κόπτειν διαφέρε; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.147; δ. τὰς μορφάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 497b15}}; δ. εἴς τι, ἔν τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.2, 7; παρὰ τὴν Βεβρυκίαν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 1; καθ᾽ ὑπεροχὴν καὶ ἕλλειψιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 486a22; κατὰ τὴν θέσιν [prev. author] “Mete.” 341b24; πρός τι [prev. author] “HA” 505a21; τίνι δ. τὰ ἄρρενα τῶν θηλειῶν.. θεωρείσθω [prev. author] “PA” 684b3}}: with _infinitive_, μόνῃ τῇ μορφῇ μὴ οὑχὶ πρόβατα εἶναι δ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 15: with _Article_, τρεῖς μόναι ψῆφοι διήνεγκαν τὸ μὴ θανάτου τιμῆσαι}} three votes **made the difference** (i.e. majority) against capital punishment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.167}}; also διαφέρει τὸ ἥμισυ τοῦ ἔργου **makes a difference** equal to half the effort expended, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.17.}}
**III.2** _impersonal_, διαφέρει **it makes a difference,** πλεῖστον δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.22; βραχὺ δ. τοῖς θανοῦσιν εἰ.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1248, etc.}}; οὐδὲν δ. **it makes** no **odds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 193; σμικρὸν οἴει διαφέρει; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467c: with _dative_ _person_, δ. μοι}} **it makes a difference** to me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 316b, etc.}}; ἰδίᾳ τι αὐτῷ δ. he **has** some private **interest at stake,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42}}; εἰ ὑμῖν μή τι δ. if you **see** no **objection,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187d; τί δέ σοι τοῦτο δ. εἴτε.. εἴτε μ; [prev. author] “R.” 349a, compare “Grg.” 497b, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, οὐδέ τί οἱ διέφερεν ἀποθανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85}}: with personal _construction_, πράγματά τινι διαφέροντα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 65}}; **to be of importance,** πρός or εἴς τι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.420,428; τῷ ζῴῳ [prev. author] “UP” 9.5.}}
**III.3** τὸ δ. **the difference, the odds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 45d}}; ={τὸ συμφέρον} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 31; περὶ μεγίστων δὴ τῶν -όντων βουλεύεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.92, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.5, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.12}}; τὰ ἀναγκαιότερα τῷ ταμιείῳ δ. vital **interests,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PThead. 15.17 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὸ δ. μέρος τῶν ἀποφάσεων the **essential** part, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1204.11 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὰ δ. **vital** matters, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Rom.2.18; ἐπιστάμενος τὰ δ. παραβαίνειν τολμᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.19 (but τὰ δ.}} also simply, **points of difference,** in character and the like, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, etc.).}}
**III.4** **to be different from** a person: generally, **in point of excess, surpass, excel** him (compare above [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.8), τινός variant for{-όντως} in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}]; τινί **in** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.39, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 36.6; ἔν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.39; εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35b; κατὰ μέγεθος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.10; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.181}}: with _infinitive_, δ. τινὸς μεταβιβάζειν τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517b: sometimes followed by ἤ, πολὺ διέφερεν ἀλέξασθαι ἤ..}} **it was** far **better..** than.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.33, compare “Mem.” 3.11.14, “Vect.” 4.25}} (where it means **to differ in point of diminution**); also δ. μέγα τι παρὰ τὰς ἄλλας πόλεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.27.5}}: _absolutely_, **excel,** ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.12; τάχει 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.53c; οἱ τόποι διαφέρουσι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.14.9; διαφέρον τι πεπραχέναι}}**a remarkable achievement,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.39.2.}}
**III.5** **prevail,** ἐπὶ πολὺ διήνεγκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83.}}
**III.6** **quarrel, struggle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 20; οἱ διαφέροντες}}**the parties, litigants,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PPar. 69 B10 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III.7** **come between, intervene,** ὁ διαφέρων χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.94.}}
**III.8** **belong to,** τινί, as property, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.207, “PLond.” 3.940.23 (3rd c.AD)}}; of persons, **belong to** a household, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 26.5 (4th c.AD)}}; οἱ -φέροντες **kinsfolk,** {{Refs|Annuario 4 /5.476 (from Bargylia)}}; **appertain to,** τῇ ὠνῇ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1062.21 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὰ εἰς τοῦτο -φέροντα πράγματα Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 372v3 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** _middle_ and _passive_, **be at variance, quarrel,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 72, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 32, etc.; περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 7b}}; δ. ἀλλήλοις **differ with,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.42; τινὶ περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.4; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.17, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Oxy.” 1607 “fragment” 1 iii 60, etc.; τὰ πρὸς ἀλλήλους “Supp.epigram” 1.363.5 (Samos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἀμφί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.17; διενεχθέντας γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220}}; δ. ὡς.. **maintain on the contrary** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.46}}; οὐ διαφέρομαι, ={οὔ μοι διαφέρει}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.8}}; μηδὲν διὰ τοῦτο διαφέρου **let there be** no **dispute** on this ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.17}}; οἱ -φερόμενοι **the litigants**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 685.29 (Crete, 2nd c.BC).}} —Not in _Epic dialect_

1309:
** δια-φεύγω**,
_future_ **-φεύξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135d}}:—**get away from, escape,** τινά or τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204, 3.19, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.90, etc.; θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39a; κίνδυνον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.6}}; **survive,** [νόσημα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603b11; γάμον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 21: _absolutely_, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, etc.; μὴ.. ἀθῷος διαφύγῃ “PTeb.” 44.28 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐκ τῆς Μήλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.39; δ. ἐκ πόνων εἰς ἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 815e}}; διαφεύγει δ᾽ οὐδὲ νῦν but **it is** not even now **too late,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.31}}:—_passive_, διεφεύχθη ὁ κίνδυνος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.9.}}
**2** **escape one's notice** or **memory,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95e, “Men.” 96e; σὲ διαφεύξεται ἡ ἀλήθεια [prev. author] “Prm.” 135d; δ. τὰ νολλὰ τῶν νοσημάτων αὐτούς 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.228a; πολλά με διαπέφευγε 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.187.}}

1310:
** διαφημίζω**,
**make known, spread abroad,** {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.46, NT.Mark.1.45; ὡς.. 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 13}}:—_passive_, with _infinitive_, διεφημίσθη θνῄσκειν ὁ βασιλεύς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.33.3}}: _absolutely_, **to be celebrated,** ἐπὶ ταῖς καλοκἀγαθίαις {{Refs|Vett. Val. 250.5}}:—_middle_, _aorist 1_ διεφημίξαντο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 26.}}
**II** **call, name,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 221}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 442 (variant), 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 50.}}

1311:
** διαφθείρω**,
_future_ **-φθερῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 438, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ **-φθέρσω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.625}}: _perfect_ διέφθαρκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 226, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30d, etc.; also διέφθορα (see. below 111)}}:— _passive_, _future_ διαφθᾰρήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.37}}; _Ionic dialect_ διαφθερέομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.108, 9.42}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ διεφθάρατο {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.90}}:—**destroy utterly,** πόλιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.625; ἔργα διαφθείρεσκε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36}}; **make away with, kill,** τινά {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.88, etc.}}; **destroy, ruin,** ἥδ᾽ ἡμέρα φύσει σε καὶ διαφθερεῖ S.[same place]; τὴν τύχην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1069}}; δ. χεῖρα **weaken, slacken** one's hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1055}}; **spoil, break,** ὑγιῆ λίθον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.33 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ θυρώματα διεφθάρθαι {{Refs|“IG” 22.1046.11}}; δ. τὴν συνουσίαν **break up** the party, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338d.}}
**2** in moral sense, **corrupt, ruin,** γνώμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 932}}; δ. τοὺς νέους, τοὺς νεωτέρους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30b, 25a; νεανίσκον συνὼν δ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 337}}; especially **corrupt by bribes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.51; ἀργυρίῳ δ. τινά 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.9; διαφθειρομένων ἐπὶ χρήμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.45}}; δ. γυναῖκα **seduce** a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.16, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 318 (_passive_)}}; δ. τοὺς νόμους **falsify, counterfeit** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.11; γραμματεῖον [prev. author] 17.33 (_passive_, [prev. work]24)}}; τὰ φεφ αδηκότα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.37 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC).}}
**3** οὐδὲν διαφθείρας τοῦ χρώματος **having changed** nothing of his colour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117b.}}
**4** of a woman, **to lose by miscarriage** or **premature birth,** ἔμβρυα, βρέφος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.53, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.242c}}: _absolutely_, **miscarry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.73, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.36}}:—_passive_, τῶν διαφθαρεισῶν τὰ ἔμβρυα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.72.}}
**5** **lose, forget,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 389.}}
**6** ={διάγω}, uncertain in {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 280.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be destroyed,** δ. ἐπὶ τοῖς ἱματίοις **to be murdered** for the clothes he wore, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.5}}; of animals, frequently in Papyrus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 74.14 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; especially **to be crippled, disabled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34}}; of ships,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 166, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.142}}; **to be spoilt,** γάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“BGU” 1109.11 (1st c.BC),compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.84}}; **to be corrupted,** αἷμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.297, +others; τὴν ἀκοὴν διεφθαρμένος}}**deaf,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38}}; τὰ σκέλεα διεφθάρησαν **had their** legs **broken,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.28; διέφθαρμαι δέμας τὸ πᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1056; τὰ ὄμματα δ.}}**blinded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 517a; σὰς φρένας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1192}}; τὸ φρενῶν διαφθαρέν, ={φρενοβλάβεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 297, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.8: _absolutely_, διεφθαρμένος}} **decomposed,** of a corpse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614b.}}
**III** _perfect_ διέφθορα _intransitive_, **to have lost one's wits,** διέφθορας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.128; also in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, διεφθορὸς αἷμα}} **corrupted** blood, {{Refs|“Mul.” 2.134}}; frequently in later Prose, γάλα δ. ἤδη {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.5.4; τὰ δ. σώματα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.87c, compare 128e, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 3, etc.}}; but,
**III.2** in Trag. and Comedy texts always _transitive_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th c.AD(?): Ammonius Epigrammaticus 42, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 127), τὰς.. ἐλπίδας διέφθορεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 306; τὰς φρένας διέφθορε.. μοναρχία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1014; τὸν λόγον δ. 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 292, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus [same place], 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 490, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 3.}}
**IV** _aorist_ διέφθειρα _intransitive_, **became corrupt,** {{LXX|LXX.Josh.2.19.}}

1312:
** διαφθορ-ά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **διαφθορή**, ἡ, (διαφθείρω) **destruction, ruin,** ἐπὶ -φθορᾷ τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.86; ἀπέστειλε ἐπὶ διαφθορῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.164; μέχρι διαφθορᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242d: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 573, etc.}}
**2** **destruction, blight,** of things, ὀμμάτων διαφθοραί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 552; διαφθορὰ μορφῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 643.}}
**3** in moral sense, **corruption, seduction,** νέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 19; κριτῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1372a34 (_plural_).}}
**4** **miscarriage, abortion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.3, “Coac.” 505}}, **Melanges Holleaux** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|265 (2nd-1st c.BC).}}
**5** stomachic **disorder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.5.}}
**II** concrete, ἰχθύσιν δ. **a prey** for fishes, of a corpse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1297; πολεμίοις ὕβρισμα καὶ δ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 459.}}

1313:
** διάφορ-ος**, ον,
**different, unlike,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.83, 4.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 964a, etc.; παρά τι 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.30}}: with _genitive_, **differing from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61d, etc.}}
**b** **several, various,** κατὰ τὰς δ. ὕλας {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 24; δ. πρόσωπα “POxy.” 1033.88 (4th c.AD),cf. NT.Heb.9.13.}}
**c** **ambiguous,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** **differing** or **disagreeing with** another, πολλοῖς δ. εἰμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 579}}; especially in hostile sense, **at variance with,** Κλεομένεϊ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.75; τοῖς οἰκείοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.44}}; ἀλλήλοις, ἑαυτοῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337b, “Lg.” 679b; ἀνώμαλος καὶ δ. πρὸς ἑαυτόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 6: with _genitive_, δ. τινος}} one's **adversary,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.15, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 209.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 162.}}
**3** **excellent, distinguished, remarkable,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 175.3; δ. γλυκύτητι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.57; πρὸς ἀρετήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 16}}: _comparative_, ὄνομα {{NT|NT.Heb.1.4.}}
**4** **making a difference to one,**
**4.a** in good sense, **advantageous, profitable, important,** δ. ἑτέρου μᾶλλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.3; πρὸς σωτηρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 779b.}}
**4.b** rarely in bad sense, **disagreeable,** γείτονα γείτονι μηδὲν ποιεῖν δ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 843c.}}
**II** as _substantive_, διάφορον, τό,
**II.1** **difference,** σμικρόν τι τὸ δ. εὕροι τις ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.7}}; διάφορα πολλὰ θεῶν βροτοῖσιν εἰσορῶ I see many **differences** between gods and men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 612 (Lyric poetry)}}; μέγα τὸ δ. ἐστι (variant{διαφέρον}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14; ἆρα μικρὰ τὰ δ. ἑκατέροις τῆς οὑσία; 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.47; ἡλίκα γ᾽ ἐστὶν τὰ διάφορ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ἢ ἐκεῖ πολεμεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.27.}}
**II.2** **what concerns one,** τῶν ἡμῖν ἐς τὰ μέγιστα διαφόρων **matters of** the greatest **concern** to us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87; τηλικούτων ὄντων αὐτῷ τῶν δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.68, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352b2.}}
**II.3** **difference, disagreement,** ἕνεκα τῶν αὐτοῖς ἰδίᾳ δ. on account of their private **differences,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68, compare 2.37}}; τὸ Ἀθηναίων δ. **difference with** the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, [prev. author] 2.27.}}
**II.4** in reference to money-matters, **difference, balance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 17, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 25.4}}; **expenditure,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1251b10; ἡμικρολογία ἐστὶ φειδωλία τοῦ δ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 10.1}}: in _plural_, **expenses,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.18, “IG” 5(1).1390.45 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; **losses**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.30 (2nd c.BC), Wilcken “Chr.” 11 B 8 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.4.b** **ready money, cash,** χρείας γενομένης ἀναγκαίας τῷ δήμῳ διαφόρου {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).388.7 (from Amorgos)}}; **sum of money**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.330.8 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 3.7 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.18.8, “IG” 12(5).653.56 (Syros, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.: _plural_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.27.13, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2695 (from Iasus)}}; **interest,** ἐπὶ διαφόρῳ ἡμιολίας {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1040.8 (3rd c.AD)}}; **price,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 81, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.9.}}
**II.5** **expenses of carriage,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.69.12 (2nd c.AD), “PFay.” 86a11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** _adverb_ -ρως **with a difference,** τοῖς παροῦσιν ἤθεσι δ. πολιτεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 531b: with _genitive_, δ. τῶν λοιπῶν δένδρων “Geoponica” 10.37.1}}; **in a variety of ways,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.9}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Mus.” p.109K.}}
**III.2** δ. ἔχειν **differ,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 25e}}, etc.; δ. ἔχειν τινί **to differ with..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.18.}}
**III.3** **pre-eminently,** πρᾶος καὶ φιλάνθρωπος τῶν ἄλλων δ. ὤν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 24.196, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.9 (_superlative_)}}; δ. συναρέσκει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 333, compare “Pk.” 72}}; **excellently, with distinction,** ἀγωνίσασθαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Sosyl. p.30B.; δ. ἀπειργασμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.7.2}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1314:
** διαφῠλάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **διαφυλάττω**, Cretan dialect **διαφυλάδδω** (written διαφυλάδω), {{Refs|“GDI” 5169.11, +others}}:— **watch closely, guard carefully,** τὰ τείχεα, τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101, 133; τὴν πάροδον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.30; τὰ ἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.6, compare “SIG” 577.15 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; especially of providential care, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Psa.90(91).11, +others, compare “PGiss.” 17.7 (2nd c.AD: Hadrianus Imperator Epigrammaticus)}}, etc.:—_middle_, **guard for oneself,** πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 369.}}
**2** **observe closely,** τὰ μέτρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.ά.}}
**3** **observe, maintain,** τοὺς νόμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951b, compare “SIG” 1044.10 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 25.3 (2nd c.BC, _passive_)}}; εἰρήνην Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.78; τὴν πρός τινα πίστιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.78.8; εὔνοιαν “IG” 12(7).241.22 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC)}}; δ. τὸ μὴ σπουδάζειν **guard against** being too particular.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 261e}}; πλῆθος δ. ὅτι μάλιστα ταὐτὸν αὑτῶν εἶναι **take care** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 112d.}}
**4** **remember, retain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 1, “Cont.” 7.}}

1315:
** διαχειρ-ίζω**,
**have in hand, conduct, manage,** χρήματα, πράγματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.147, 2.17, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 526b, etc.; αἱ ἀρχαὶ δ. πολλὰ τῶν κοινῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b8; χρήματα “OGI” 218.74 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC), etc.:}} —so in _middle_, _future_ _participle_ -ιούμενος {{Refs|H. “Mul.” 2.111, etc.}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.17.}}
**II** _middle_, **lay hands on, slay,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.21.8, NT.Act.5.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.220b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 72.14.}}

1318:
** δῐδακ-τός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 398d}}:
**I** of things, **taught, learnt,** ἅπαντα γάρ σοι τἀμὰ νουθετήματα κείνης διδακτά **of** her **teaching,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 344; δ. ἀνθρωπίνης σοφίας λόγοι NT.1Cor.2.13; ὅσοις δ. μηδέν, ἀλλ᾽ ἐν τῇ φύσει τὸ σωφρονεῖν εἴληχεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 79.}}
**I.2** **that can be taught** or **learnt,** τὰ δ. **things which may be taught by study and experience,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.41}}; opposed to ἄρρητα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 300}}; δίδαξον.. εἰ διδακτά μοι if I **may learn** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 64, compare 671; τὰ μὲν δ. μανθάνω, τὰ δ᾽ εὑρετὰ ζητῶ [prev. author] “fragment” 843}}; κἄστ᾽ οὐ διδακτόν (i.e. τὸ τῆς τύχης) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 786, compare “Supp.” 914; καθ᾽ ὅσον δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.20; ἀρετὴν.. εἴτε δ. εἴ τε μὴ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71a, compare “Prt.” 328c, “Euthd.” 274e; ἐπιστήμη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139b25.}}
**II** of persons, **taught, instructed,** πολέμου {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.4.7}}; also δ. θεοῦ **taught** by God,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.54.13 (= NT.John.6.45).}}

1319:
** δῐδασκᾰλ-ία**, ἡ,
**teaching, instruction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.102, 5th c.BC: Evenus Elegiacus 1, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493b, etc.}}; δ. ποιεῖσθαι, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42}}; δ. παρέχειν serve as a **lesson,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 87}}; ἐκ δ., opposed to ἐξ ἔθους, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103a15.}}
**2** **elucidation,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1456b5.}}
**3** **official instructions,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PLips. 64.24 (4th c.AD)}}; πρὸς διδασκαλίαν for **information,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 1101.4 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** **training, rehearsing** of a chorus, etc., δ. τῶν χορῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501e, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 147.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1096a, etc.}}; also, **the dramas produced,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.839d, “Cim.” 8, “Per.” 5, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.37 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** διδασκαλίαι, αἱ, **Catalogues of the Dramas,** their writers, dates, and success, title of compilation by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus and others, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.26, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1155, etc.}}

1320:
** δῐδάσκᾰλ-ος**, ὁ
(but _feminine_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 556, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 110}}, compare ξυμφορὴ γίνεται δ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 76; πενία ἐπινοιῶν δ. 2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 10)}}, **teacher, master,** μαντείης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” [same place]; δ. τέχνης πάσης βροτοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” [same place]; δεινῶν ἔργων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.78; πόλεμος βίαιος δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; διδάσκαλον λαβεῖν get **a master,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” 1120.8}}; εἰς διδασκάλου (i.e. οἶκον) φοιτᾶν go to **school,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.109d, etc.}}; διδασκάλων or ἐκ διδασκάλων ἀπαλλαγῆναι leave **school,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 514c, “Prt.” 326c}}; ἐν διδασκάλων at **school,** {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.110b.}}
**II** **trainer** of a dithyrambic or dramatic chorus, **producer** of a play, etc., ἴτω δὲ καὶ τραγῳδίας ὁ Κλεομάχου δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 256, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 912, “Ach.” 628, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.13, etc.; δ. τοῦ μεγάλου χοροῦ “SIG” 698.8 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}

1321:
** δῐδάσκω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ -έμεναι and -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.442, 23.308}}: _future_ διδάξω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 519, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐδίδαξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.307, etc.; poetry ἐδιδάσκησα “hymnus ad Cererem” 144 (probably), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 64, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.217}}: _perfect_ δεδίδαχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85e:}} —_middle_, _future_ διδάξομαι: _aorist_ ἐδιδαξάμην:—_passive_, _future_ διδαχθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.70, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐδιδάχθην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.51, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 637, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδίδαγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.831, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 269c, etc. Redupl. form of δάω (which see)}} in causal sense:—**instruct** a person, or **teach** a thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.832, 9.442: with double _accusative_, σε.. ἱπποσύνας ἐδίδαξαν}} they **taught** thee riding,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.307, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.481; πολλὰ διδάσκει μ᾽ ὁ πολὺς βίοτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 252 (Lyric poetry), etc.; also δ. τινὰ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 382; δ. τῶν γενομένων τισὶ τὴν ἀλήθειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201b: with _accusative_ _person_ et}}_infinitive_, σε διδάσκουσιν θεοὶ αὐτοὶ ὑψαγόρην ἔμεναι **teach** thee to be.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.384: with}}_infinitive_ only, δίδαξε γὰρ Ἄρτεμις αὐτὴ βάλλειν ἄγρια πάντα she **taught how** to shoot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.51, etc.}}: without _infinitive_, πολλοὶ τοὺς υἱοὺς ῥήτορας διδάσκουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristonymus Comicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.4.105; δ. πολλοὺς αὐλητάς 5th c.BC: Charon Historicus 9; τούτους ἱππέας ἐδίδαξεν οὐδενὸς χείρους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 94b; also δ. τινὰ σοφόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 575}}: with an abstract subject, πολυμαθίη νόον οὐ διδάσκει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 40; ξενιτείη αὐτάρκειαν δ. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 246}}:—_middle_, **teach oneself, learn,** φθέγμα καὶ ἀστυνόμους ὀργὰς ἐδιδάξατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 356 (Lyric poetry)}}; but usually, **have** one **taught** or **educated,** especially of a father, τὰ ἄλλα.. διδάσκεσθαι τοὺς ὑεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325b; δ. τοὺς ὑεῖς τὰς κούφας ἐργασίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321a24}}: with _infinitive_, δ. τινὰ ἱππεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467e; δ. τινα ἱππέα [prev. author] “Men.” 93d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.5}} (this distinction between _active_ and _middle_ was neglected by some Poets and late Prose writers, _middle_ being used like _active_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.59, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 10, etc.; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 783 Elmsl. restored διδάξαιμ᾽ ἄν σ᾽ ἔτι for διδαξαίμην σ᾽ ἔτι, and in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421e Cobet conjecture διδάξει for -εται}}: _middle_ is used of gods, [θεοί].. ὅπλων χρῆσιν διδαξάμενοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mx.” 238b)}}:—_passive_, **to be taught, learn,** with _genitive_, διδασκόμενος πολέμοιο **trained, skilled** in war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.811: with _accusative_, τά σε προτί φασιν Ἀχιλλῆος δεδιδάχθαι}} which [medicines] they say thou **wert taught** by Achilles,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.831, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 529; ὃς οὔτ᾽ ἐδιδάχθη οὔτε εἶδε καλὸν οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81; διδάξω καὶ διδάξομαι λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 739}}: frequently with _infinitive_, δεδιδαγμένον εἶναι χειροήθεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.69; βρέφος διδάσκεται λέγειν ἀκούειν θ᾽ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 914}}; διδάσκεσθαι ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.45.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **indicate, give sign of,** χειμῶνος συναγειρομένοιο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 793, compare 734.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **explain,** πῶς δ; δίδαξον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 431; σαφῶς δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, etc.}}; **show by argument, prove,** λέγων διδασκέτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.11, etc.}}; δ. περί τινος ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.71; ἡλίκον ἐστὶ τὸ ἀλαζόνευμα.. πειράσομαι.. διδάξαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.238}}; ποιητὴς δ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.50b.}}
**III** of dithyrambic and dramatic Poets {{Refs|compare (διδάσκαλος 11), δ. διθύραμβον, δρᾶμα}} **produce** a piece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23, 6.21; Πέρσας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1026, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327d, “IG” 12.770, +others:}} —_middle_, διδάξασθαι χορόν **train one's own** chorus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 145.}}

1322:
** δῐδᾰχή**, ἡ,
**teaching,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 33, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 536d; ἐκ διδαχῆς λέγειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134; δ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126.—poetical only late, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 89.}}
**2** military **regulations** or **discipline,** τοὐναντίον αὐτῶν τῆς στρατιωτικῆς δ. πεποιηκότων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 140.16 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** ={διδασκαλία}{{Refs|11.2, “IG” 14.2124.}}

1323:
** δῐδραχμ-ος**, ον,
**priced at two drachms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1353a17}}; δ. ὁπλῖται soldiers **with pay of two drachms a day,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3. [17].}}
**II** **weighing two drachms,** ὁλκή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudemus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.185.}}
**III** δ. τόκος interest **at two drachms** per mina per month (24%), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1146.38 (Gythium, 1st c.BC), “BGU” 1126.17 (1st c.BC).}}
**IV** δίδραχμον, τό, **coin of two drachms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.79, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 10.7, etc.}}: especially **half-shekel,** paid to the temple-treasury at Jerusalem, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.3.47, +others (frequently with see l. δίδραγμον, which see), NT.Matt.17.24.}}

1324:
** δίδῠμος** [ῐ], η, ον,
also ος, ον variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.209, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 656 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113e:}} —_reduplicate_ from δύο, **double, twofold,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.227, etc.; διδύμαιν χειροῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 206 (Lyric poetry): also in _sing._, χερὶ διδύμᾳ}} with **both** hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.9}}; δ. ἅλς, i. e. the Pontus and Bosporus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 967 (Lyric poetry); δ. γένος “Anthologia Graeca” 7.72 (4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus); δ. ξύλον}}**forked,** {{LXX|LXX.Josh.8.29}}; τὸ γλυκύ μοι δ., of a wife, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1974.}}
**II** **twin,** δ. κασίγνητος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.36; δ. τέκνων ἄριστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1693 (Lyric poetry); δ. τέκεα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 220 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** _substantive_, δίδυμοι **twins,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.641, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.41}}: of **the Twins** in the zodiac, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 147, “IG” 14.1307}}; also δίδυμα, τά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52; δύο διδύμω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1401 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.2** **the testicles,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Deu.25.11, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.125 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus): _sing._, 3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 14.11.}}
**III.3** **ovaries,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus [same place], 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.12.}}

1325:
** δίδωμι**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.620, etc.}} (late **δίδω** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 121 (3rd c.AD))}}; late forms, _1st pers._ _plural_ διδόαμεν see reading in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.8.5, etc., _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δίδωσι (παρα-) [prev. author] “AJ” 10.4.1, etc.}}; but thematic forms are frequently used, especially in _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_, διδοῖς, διδοῖσθα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.164, 19.270}}, διδοῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.350, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.16, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 12 (ἀνα-), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1010, etc., διδοῦσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.265 (always in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, uncertain in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 156; _imperative_ δίδου 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1303, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 642, δίδοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, epigram in “Class.Phil.” 4.78}}, _Epic dialect_ δίδωθι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.380}}; _infinitive_ διδόναι, also διδοῦν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1329}}, _Epic dialect_ διδοῦναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.425}}, _Aeolic dialect_ δίδων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.9}}; _participle_ διδούς, _Aeolic dialect_ δίδοις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 23.13:}}_imperfect_ ἐδίδουν -ους -ου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 678, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.367, 11.289}} (_Epic dialect_ δίδου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.165), etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐδίδοσαν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.9, etc., ἐδίδουν (variant{ἐδίδων}) 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 139, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.6}}] codices (ἀπ-), also ἔδιδον probably in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 437, δίδον [prev. work] 328}}; _Epic dialect_ _iterative_ δόσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.382}}: _future_ δώσω {{Refs|14.268, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ διδώσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.358, 24.314}}; _infinitive_ δωσέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.369:}}_aorist 1_ ἔδωκα, used only in _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.361, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ δῶκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.43:}}_aorist 2_ ἔδων, used in _plural_ _indicative_ ἔδομεν ἔδοτε ἔδοσαν (_Laconian dialect_ ἔδον {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1 B 1)}}, and in moods, δός, δῶ, δοίην, δοῦναι, δού; _Epic dialect_ forms of _aorist_, _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δώῃ, δώῃσι, δῷσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.725, 1.324, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.144}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δώη, _Boeotian dialect_ δώει {{Refs|“SIG” 2858.17 (Delph.), “IG” 7.3054 (Lebad.), δοῖ “PPetr.” 2. p.24}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ δώομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.299, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.184}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δώωσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.137}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ is written δόη {{Refs|“UPZ” 1.4, δοῖ “IG” 14.1488, etc.}}; _infinitive_ δόμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.116, δόμεν 4.379}} (also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1163 (ἀπο-), δόμειν “SIG” 942 (from Dodona)); 5th c.AD: Cypria}} _infinitive_ δοϝέναι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.5H. (also _optative_ δυϝάνοι [prev. work] 6); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _participle_ ἀπυ-δόας {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).6.13 (from Tegea)}}; _infinitive_ δῶναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 666.2 (Orchom., 3rd c.BC)}}, also in later Greek, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 38.13 (2nd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ δέδωκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.8, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἀπο-δεδόανθι {{Refs|“IG” 7.3171.35 (Orchom.)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐδεδώκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.26}}:—_middle_ only in compounds:— _passive_, _future_ δοθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1650, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.39, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐδόθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78, etc.}}: _perfect_ δέδομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.428, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1041, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δέδονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 757}}: _pluperfect_ ἐδέδοτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109}}:—**give freely,** τινί τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.274, etc.}}: in _present_ and _imperfect_, **to be ready to give, offer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.519, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94, 9.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 100, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.9, etc.; τὰ διδόμενα}}**things offered,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.119.}}
**2** of the gods, **grant, assign,** κῦδος, νίκην, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.204, 11.397}}, etc.; of evils, δ. ἄλγεα, ἄτας, κήδεα, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.96, 19.270, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.15, etc.; twice in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in _passive_, οὔ τοι δέδοται πολεμήϊα ἔργα not to thee **have** deeds of war **been granted,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.428, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78}}; later εὖ διδόναι τινί **give** good fortune, **provide** well **for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1081, “OC” 642, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 750}}: _absolutely_, of the laws, **grant permission,** δόντων αὐτῷ τῶν νόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 813c.}}
**3** **offer** to the gods, ἑκατόμβας, ἱρὰ θεοῖσιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.6, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.67, etc.}}
**4** with _infinitive_ added, ξεῖνος γάρ οἱ ἔδωκεν.. ἐς πόλεμον φορέειν **gave** it him to wear in war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.532, compare 23.183; δῶκε [τεύχεα] θεράποντι φορῆναι 7.149}}: later frequently of **giving** to eat or drink, ἐκ χειρὸς διδοῖ πιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172, compare 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 124, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 69, etc.; ἐδίδου ῥοφεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 203; δίδου μασᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 253; δὸς καταφαγεῖν 5th c.BC: Hegemon Comicus et Parodus 1; τὴν κύλικα δὸς ἐμπιεῖν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 41; δὸς τὴν μεγάλην σπάσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 17.7}}; with _infinitive_ omitted, φιάλην ἔδωκε κεράσας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 10; εὐζωρότερον δός 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 58}}; also of **giving** water to wash with, δίδου κατὰ χειρός (i.e. νίψασθαι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Archedicus Comicus 2.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.2.}}
**5** Prose phrases, δ. ὅρκον, opposed to λαμβάνειν, **tender** an oath, δοκεῖ κἂν ὀμόσαι εἴ τις αὐτῷ ὅρκον διδοίη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.24, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377a8}}; δ. ψῆφον, γνώμην, **put** a proposal to the vote, **propose** a resolution, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87, 24.13: δ. χάριν, ={χαρίζεσθαι}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1354, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 317}}]; ὀργῇ χάριν δούς **having indulged**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 855}}; λόγον τινὶ δ. **give** one leave to speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.20; δ. λόγον σφίσι}}**deliberate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97; οὐκ, εἰ διδοίης.. σαυτῷ λόγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 583}}; δοῦναι, λαβεῖν λόγον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 165a27 (but δ. λόγον, εὐθύνας}} **render** accounts, [{{Refs|“IG” 12.91, +others): δ. δίκην or δίκας, see at {δίκη}: ἀκοὴν δ. λόγοις}}] **lend** an ear to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 30, etc.}}; δ. ἐργασίαν **give** diligence, = Latin _dare operam_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 441.109 (Lagina, 1st c.BC), “POxy.” 742.11}}: with _infinitive_, {{NT|NT.Luke.12.58: _absolutely_, i.e. πληγήν, λίθῳ δ. τινί “PLips.” 13 iii 3}}; ἐμβολὰς διδόναι, **ram,** of ships, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.10.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **hand over, deliver up,** ἀχέεσσί με δώσεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.167; μιν.. ὀδύνῃσιν ἔδωκεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.397; Ἕκτορα κυσίν 23.21; πυρί τινα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.65; πληγαῖς τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574c; ἔδωκε θῆρας φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.60.}}
**II.2** of parents, **give** their daughter **to wife,** θυγατέρα ἀνδρί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.7}}; also of Telemachus, ἀνέρι μητέρα δώσω {{Refs|2.223}}; τὴν.. Σάμηνδε ἔδοσαν **gave** her **in marriage** to go to Samé, {{Refs|15.367, compare 17.442}}; with _infinitive_ added, δώσω σοι Χαρίτων μίαν ὀπυιέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.268: in Prose and Trag., θυγατέρα δ. τινὶ γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.4, etc.: _absolutely_, ἐδίδοσαν καὶ ἤγοντο ἐξ ἀλλήλων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.β,compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 288; also δ. κόρᾳ ἄνδρα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.117.}}
**II.3** διδόναι τινά τινι **grant** another to one's entreaties, **pardon** him at one's request, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.31}}; διδόναι τινί τι **forgive** one a thing, **condone** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 296 (assuming variant).}}
**II.4** δ. ἑαυτόν τινι **give** oneself **up,** δ. σφέας αὐτοὺς τοῖσι Ἀθηναίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 84, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.68; τινὶ εἰς χεῖρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1348; δ. ἑαυτὸν τοῖς δεινοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.97; εἰς τοὺς κινδύνους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.17.8; εἰς ἔντευξιν [prev. author] 3.15.4}}; εἰς τρυφήν, εἰς λῃστείας, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.108, 18.47}}: with _infinitive_, δίδωσ᾽ ἑκὼν κτείνειν ἑαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1341.}}
**II.5** **appoint, establish,** of a priest, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.31.6}}; δῶμεν ἀρχηγόν{{LXX|LXX.Num.14.4}}; δ. τινὰ εἰς ἔθνος μέγα{{LXX|LXX.Gen.17.20}}; **place,** τινὰ ὑπεράνω πάντα τὰ ἔθνη{{LXX|LXX.Deu.28.1:—}}_passive_, οἱ δεδομένοι, = **Nethinim, ministers** of the Temple,{{LXX|LXX.Neh.5.3}}; ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ἵνα.. **orders were given** them that.., {{NT|NT.Rev.9.5.}}
**III** in vows and prayers, with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **grant, allow, bring about that**.., especially in prayers, δὸς ἀποφθίμενον δῦναι δόμον Ἄϊδος εἴσω **grant that** he may go.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.322; τὸν κασίγνητον δότε τυίδ᾽ ἴκεσθαι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.2}}; δός με τείσασθαι **give** me **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 18, compare “Eu.” 31; also with _dative_ _person_, τούτῳ.. εὐτυχεῖν δοῖεν θεοί [prev. author] “Th.” 422; θεοὶ δοῖέν ποτ᾽ αὐτοῖς.. παθεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 316, compare “OC” 1101, 1287, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 737b.}}
**III.2** **grant, concede** in argument, δ. καὶ συγχωρεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 100b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 990a12, +others}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 239b29; δ. εἶναι θεούς 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.3; ἑνὸς ἀτόπου δοθέντος τἆλλα συμβαίνει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 186a9}}; δεδομένα, τά, **data,** title of work by Euclid; ἡ δοθεῖσα γραμμή, γωνία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87a, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.9, etc.; δεδόσθω κύκλος 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.6, +others}}; also in Alchemy, δός **take** certain substances, {{Refs|“Pleid.X.” 69.}}
**IV** Grammars, **describe, record,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.93, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.207.}}
**V** seemingly _intransitive_, **give oneself up, devote oneself,** with _dative_, especially ἡδονῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 13; ἡδοναῖς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.12; ἐλπίδι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.12.2; εἰς δημοκοπίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 25.8; δρόμῳ δοὺς φέρεσθαι}}**at full speed,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.47.}}

1326:
** διεγ-είρω**,
**wake up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.47, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.13.7, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.1.5}}; **stir up, arouse,** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.7.21}}; **excite, promote,** αὔξησιν φυτοῦ {{Refs|“Geoponica” 9.3.7}}:—_passive_, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 15, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 876a22, LXX.Est.11.11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.485, 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 2.35}}; **to be raised up** from a sick-bed, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.171 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ διέγρετο{{Refs|[prev. work] 5.274 (Paul. Sil.).}}
**II** **raise,** τὸν αὐχένα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.4; χώματα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.1.1, 6.2.7}}:—_passive_, πύλας διεγειρομένας εἰς ὕψος πηχῶν ἑβδομήκοντα {{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX.Jdt.1.4; τοῖς πηδήμασι πρὸς οὐρανὸν διεγειρεσθε μἐσον 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor ἠθοπ.ποιμένος p.137B.}}

1327:
** διέξ-οδος**, ἡ,
**outlet, passage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.51, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 684b26, etc.; ἀποκεκληϊμένου τοῦ ὕδατος τῆς δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, compare 4.140}}; διέξοδοι ὁδῶν **passage**-ways, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.199}}; ἀνέμων διέξοδοι (through the body), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 477; ὅταν πλεύμων μὴ καθαρὰς παρέχῃ τὰς δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84d, compare 91c}}; **way out from,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98; αἱ δ. τῶν ὁδῶν NT.Matt.22.9}}; of the main **roads out of** a town, {{Refs|Aristeas 105}}; δ. ὑδάτων, of a spring, {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.2.21}}; of tears,{{LXX|LXX.Psa.118(119).136.}}
**2** **pathway, orbit,** of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24}}; τρεῖς ἡλίου διέξοδοι three **days,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1086}}; of planets, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 399a3: _metaphorically_, πολλὰς φροντίδων δ. 4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 4.5}}; δ. τῶν βουλευμάτων the **paths** of his counsels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156}}; δ. τῆς φύσεως, τῆς οὐσίας, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 1.5, 12; [ὁ νοῦς] ἔχων τὴν αὐτὴν διὰ τῶν οὐκ αὐτῶν δ. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.13.}}
**3** **issue, event,** δ. λαβεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.1.3, etc.}}
**4** **means of escape,** πάσας δ. διεξελθών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405c}}; δ. πραγμάτων **way out of** difficulties, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.66.}}
**5** in Medicine texts, **evacuation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).132 (_plural_).}}
**II** **detailed narrative** or **description,** ἡ τοῦ λόγου δ. **the course** of the narrative or argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109a, compare “Prt.” 361d, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.250, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.407}}; **exposition,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 38, “Mus.” p.110 K., +others}}; ἡ διὰ στοιχείου δ. **description** by resolving into elements, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 207c}}; κατὰ διέξοδον in **detail,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 1p.505S.; δ. καὶ ἔπαινοι}}**narratives, tales,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, etc.}}
**III** **military evolution,** δ. τακτικαί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 813e, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.5.}}
**III.2** **excursion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a.}}
**III.3** **repeated experiment,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.169.}}

1330:
** διέρχομαι**,
_future_ διελεύσομαι (but δίειμι is used in _Attic dialect_ as _future_, and διῄειν as _imperfect_): _aorist_ διῆλθον:— **go through, pass through,** _absolutely_, ἀντικρὺ δὲ διῆλθε βέλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.876, etc.: with _genitive_, φάτο.. ἔγχος ῥέα διελεύσεσθαι.. Αἰνείαο 20.263, compare 100; σφαγῶν διελθὼν ἰός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 717; δ. διὰ τῆς νήσου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.31}}; διέρχεται ἅπαντα διὰ τούτου {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 181; δ. διὰ πάντων NT.Act.9.32; εἰ σῶμα οὖσα ἡ ψυχὴ.. διῆλθε διὰ παντός 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.8: with _accusative_, δ. πῶϋ, ἄστυ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.198, 6.392}}; θύρας (_plural_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.16; τὴν πολεμίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.64; τρεῖς σταθμούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.8.}}
**2** **pass through, complete,** τὸ πέμπτον μέρος τῆς ὁδοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25; τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365b, etc.; παιδείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.1.}}
**3** of reports, βάξις διῆλθ᾽ Ἀχαιούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 999: _absolutely_, διῆλθεν ὁ λόγος}}**went abroad, spread,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7; κληδὼν γῆς διῆλθε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 256.}}
**4** of pain, **shoot through** one, {{Refs|[prev. work] 743}}; of passion, ἵμερος δ. Ἡρακλῆ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 477}}; ἐμὲ διῆλθέ τι a thought **shot through** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 288.}}
**5** **pass through and reach, arrive at,** βίου τέλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).5.}}
**6** **go through in detail, recount,** λόγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 4.72; χρησμόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 874}}; ἃ διῆλθον the **details** I **have gone through,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21}}; ὀλίγα διελθών a little **further on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344b; δ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.66, 9.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347a; ὑπέρ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.13.10; πάντα μετὰ φρεσί “hymnus ad Venerem” 276; πρὸς αὑτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.47; δ. τίς πολιτεία.. συμφέρει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296b14.}}
**II** _intransitive_ of Time, **pass, elapse,** χρόνου οὐ πολλοῦ διελθόντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, compare 3.152, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.153, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.10.17}}; τοῦ διεληλυθότος ἔτους the **past** year, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 410.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; διελθουσῶν τῶν σπονδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115; διελθὼν ἐς βραχὺν χρόνον}}**having waited,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 957 codices (perhaps ὡς).}}

1331:
** διερωτ-άω**,
**cross-question,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, “Grg.” 458a, etc.; δ. τινά τι [prev. author] “Prt.” 315c.}}
**II** **ask constantly** or **continually,** οἱ διερωτῶντες ὑμᾶς.. τί βούλεσθ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.22.}}

1332:
** δι-ετής**, ές,
or **διέτης**, ες, **of** or **lasting two years,** χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, etc.; κύησις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 777b15, etc.; ἀρξάμενος ἀπὸ διετοῦς [prev. author] “HA” 500a11}}; ἐπὶ διετὲς ἡβᾶν to be **two years** past puberty, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.12, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.122, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.20.}}
**II** **two years old,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 545b11.}}
**III** = {διετήσιος}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1337:
** δῐ-θάλασσος**,
_Attic dialect_ **διθάλαττος** [θᾰ], ον, **divided into two seas,** of the Euxine, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.22, compare 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 156}}; of the Atlantic, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.8.}}
**II** **between two seas, where two seas meet,** as is often the case off a headland, {{NT|NT.Act.27.41}}; βραχέα καὶ διθάλαττα shallows and **meetings of currents,** in the Syrtes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 5.9.}}

1338:
** διϊκνέομαι**,
_future_ -ίξομαι: _aorist_ -ικόμην:— **go through, penetrate,** ποτὶ τὰν ψυχάν δι᾽ ὤτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101a; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον -εῖται τὸ ὕδωρ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.20.4; διῖκτο ἡ δόξα μέχρι βασιλέως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 20, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 12.35}}; **reach,** with missiles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.79; βραδέως τοῦ παραγγέλματος διικνουμένου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 27.}}
**2** in speaking, **go through, tell of,** πάντα δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.61, 19.186, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.411.}}
**3** of Time, **intervene,** διετοῦς χρόνου διικνουμένου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.4.}}

1339:
** διΐστημι**,
_future_ διαστήσω, **set apart, separate,** τοὺς λόχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74; κατ᾽ εἴδη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23d; διέστησεν [αὐτοὺς] εἰς μέρη πολλά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.61; ζῶντας ἡμᾶς οὐθὲν ἀλλήλων διέστησε 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 84}}:—_passive_, κίονες διεστάθησαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2.}}
**2** **set** one **at variance with** another, τινά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 41, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.77}}; δ. τὴν Ἑλλάδα **divide** it **into factions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.2; δ. τοὺς πένητας ἀπὸ τῶν εὐπόρων 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.17.}}
**3** μέσας διαστήσας ἡμέρας δύο **having left an interval of** two days, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 996.7, compare “BKT” 3.20.}}
**4** **distinguish,** τί τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.305d, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.2.}}
**5** **inflate,** κενοὺς ἀσκούς {{Refs|Demophilus Gnomologus “Sim.” 57.}}
**II** more frequently in _passive_, with _aorist_ 2, _perfect_, and _pluperfect_ _active_:—**stand apart, be divided,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, mostly in}}_aorist_ 2, {{Refs|24.718, +others: once in _imperfect_ _middle_, θάλασσα διΐστατο}} the sea **made way, opened,**{{Refs|13.29; διαστὰν γῆς βάθρον}}**yawning wide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1662; τὰ διεστεῶτα ὑπὸ σεισμοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.129}}; διεστῶτα, opposed to ἡνωμένα, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.124, +others}}; ἔτους διεστῶτος **after an interval of** a year, {{Refs|“SIG” 344.119 (from Teos).}}
**II.b** **stand with legs apart,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ner.” 7.}}
**II.2** of persons, **stand apart, be at variance,** διαστήτην ἐρίσαντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.6; εἴ τινές που διασταῖεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18}}; διέστη ἐς ξυμμαχίαν ἑκατέρων **sided** with one or the other party,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 15; κατὰ πόλεις διέσταμεν [prev. author] 4.61; διεστηκότες εἰς δύο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.4, compare 18.18; ἐρίζειν καὶ διεστάναι [prev. author] 2.29}}; simply, **differ, be different,** πλούτου ἀρετὴ διέστηκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550e; πρὸς ἄλληλα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1256a29, compare “Po.” 1448a17; ἡ ἀριστοκρατία διέστηκεν ἀπὸ ταύτης πολὺ τῆς πολιτείας [prev. author] “Pol.” 1289b3; οὖρα διεστηκότα}}**not homogeneous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.33.}}
**II.3** **part** after fighting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, 8.16, 18}}: hence, **to be reconciled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.38.}}
**II.3.b** of an army, **retire,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.3.6.}}
**II.4** **stand at certain distances** or **intervals,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66}}; of guards in a row, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.72}}; of post-stations, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.98}}; of soldiers, δ. κατὰ διακοσίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.32}}; διάστηθι **mark distances!** a word of command, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 12.11: Geometry texts, ἴσα ἀπ᾽ ἀλλάλων διέστακεν}} **are** equi**distant** from one another, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.6.}}
**III** _middle_, sometimes _transitive_, **separate,** γεώδη γένη διϊστάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63c}}: chiefly in _aorist_ 1, δ. τόν τε δικαιότατον καὶ τὸν ἀδικώτατον **contrast,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 360e}}; ἀράχνια, of spiders, **spread,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.97.}}

1340:
** διϊσχῡρ-ίζομαι**,
**lean upon, rely on,** τῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.33, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.1.}}
**II** **affirm confidently,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63c, etc.; δ. ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχειν [prev. work] 114d}}; δ. ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 154a; δ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.4, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.85; τι ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 86b}}; περὶ σοῦ ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 317c: _absolutely_, ὁμοίως ἐφ᾽ ἑκατέροις δ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 158d, etc.}}

1342:
** δίκαιος** [ῐ], α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 901 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 1202, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.72}}: (δίκη):
**A** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and all writers, of persons, **observant of custom** or **rule,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.52}}; especially **of social rule, well-ordered, civilized,** ὑβρισταί τε καὶ ἄγριοι οὐδὲ δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.175, compare 8.575; [Γαλακτοφάγοι] δικαιότατοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.6; [Χείρων] δικαιότατος Κενταύρων 11.832, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 314, 794}}; δ. πολίτης a **good** citizen, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.21, etc.}}: _metaphorically_ of the sea, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 12.2 (_superlative_)}}; δικαίη ζόη a **civilized** way of living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.177.}} _adverb_ δικαίως, μνᾶσθαι woo **in due form, decently,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.90; ὑπὸ ζυγῷ λόφον δ. εἶχον}}**loyally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 292.}}
**A.2** **observant of duty** to gods and men, **righteous,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.209, etc.; δ. πρὸς πᾶσαν ὁμιλίην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 2; ἰθὺς καὶ δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; opposed to δυσσεβής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 598, compare 610; δ. καὶ ὅσιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507b; δικαίων ἀδίκους φρένας παρασπᾷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 791 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of actions, etc., **righteous,** ἐπὶ ῥηθέντι δικαίῳ a thing **rightly** said, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.414, etc.}}
**A.3** ὁ δίκαιος, euphemistic of a sacred snake, {{Refs|“GDI” 5056 (from Crete).}}
**B** later:
**B.I** **equal, even, well-balanced,** ἅρμα δίκαιον **evengoing** chariot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.26: so _metaphorically_, νωμᾷ δικαίῳ πηδαλίῳ στρατόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86; δικαιόταται ἀντιρροπαί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7}}; δικαιότατα μοχλεύειν {{Refs|[prev. passage]: hence}}**fair, impartial,** βάσανος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.8; συγγραφεύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 39.}}
**B.I.b** **legally exact, precise,** τῷ δικαιοτάτῳ τῶν λόγων to speak **quite exactly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.108, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44}}; of Numbers, αἱ ἑκατὸν ὀργυιαὶ δίκαιαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}. _adverb_ -αίως, πάντα δ. ὑμῖν τετήρηται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.3; δ. ἐξετάζειν [prev. work]154.}}
**B.I.2** **lawful, just,** especially τὸ δ. **right,** opposed to τὸ ἄδικον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 189 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τὸ δ. τὸ νόμιμον καὶ τὸ ἴσον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129a34; δ. διορθωτικόν, διανεμητικόν, [prev. work] 1131b25, 27}}; τὸ πολιτικὸν δ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1134b18; ἔστι ἐπιεικὲς τὸ παρὰ τὸν γεγραμμένον νόμον δ. [prev. author] “Rh.” 1374a27, compare “EN” 1137b12; καὶ δίκαια κἄδικα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 99; τὰ ἴσα καὶ τὰ δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.67}}; τοὐμὸν δ. my own **right,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 810}}; ἐλθεῖν ἐπὶ τοῦτο τὸ δ. bring the case to this **issue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.24}}; οὐδὲν τῶν δ. ποιεῖν τινί not to do **what is just and right** by a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.10}}; τὰ δ. ἔχειν, λαμβάνειν, receive **one's due,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.7.14, 17}}; τὰ δ. πράττεσθαι πόλιν give a city its **deserts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 812}}; ἐκ τοῦ δικαίου, ={δικαίως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1435, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89}}; so ἀπὸ τοῦ δικαίου, τῶν δικαίων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 50.8 (2nd c.BC), 123.8 (1st c.BC)}}; μετὰ τοῦ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.177}}; τὸ δίκαιον **lawful claim,** ἃ ἔχομεν δίκαια πρός.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.179, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 3, etc.}}; τὰ πρὸς ἀλλήλους δ. mutual **obligations** or **contracts,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.21.10}}; ἐπὶ συγκειμένοις τισὶ δικαίοις on certain agreed **terms,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.51}}. _adverb_ **-αίως** **rightly, justly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137; μεῖζον ἢ δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); καὶ δ. καὶ ἀδίκως 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.135.}}
**B.II** of persons and things, **meet and right, fitting,** δ. τοῦδε τοῦ φόνου ῥαφεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1604; κόσμος οὐ φέρειν δ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 55}}; ἵππον δ. ποιεῖσθαί τινι make a horse **fit for** another's **use,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.5, compare “Cyn.” 7.4 (ἵππος δ. τὴν σιαγόνα}} having a **good** mouth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.196).}}
**B.II.b** **normal,** σχήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 69; φύσις [prev. author] “Fract.” 1 (_superlative_).}}
**B.II.2** **real, genuine,** γόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” [1119]; ποιῶν τὰ ἐν τῇ τέχνῃ δ. “Supp.epigram” 2.184.7 (Tanagra, 2nd c.BC).}} _adverb_, εἴπερ δικαίως ἐστ᾽ ἐμός **really and truly** mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 547, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418e.}}
**B.II.3** ὁ δ. λόγος the plea **of equity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76}}. _adverb_ **-αίως** **with reason,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.34, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 675}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1149, etc.}} also **-οτέρως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.170}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1222}}; _Aeolic dialect_ δικαίτατα {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).526c17 (from Eresus).}}
**B.III** ψυχὴ ἐς τὸ δ. ἔβη 'the land of the leal', {{Refs|“IG” 7.2543.3 (from Thebes).}}
**C** in Prose, δίκαιός εἰμι, with _infinitive_, δίκαιοί ἐστε ἰέναι you **are bound** to come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, compare 8.137; δ. εἰμεν ἔχειν [prev. author] 9.27}}; δ. εἰμι κολάζειν I **have a right** to punish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1434, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 400; δ. ἐστι περιπεσεῖν κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7}}; δ. εἰσι ἀπιστότατοι εἶναι they **have most reason** to distrust, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17; δ. βλάπτεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.12; δ. ἐστιν ἀπολωλέναι}}**dignus est qui pereat,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.37}}; ὁ σπουδαῖος ἄρχειν δ. **has a right** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b12}}; with a non-personal subject, ἔλεος δ. ἀντιδίδοσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}: less frequently in _comparative_ and _superlative_, δικαιότεροι χαρίσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.34; δικαιότατος εἶ ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 172b}}; but δίκαιόν ἐστι is also found, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.39, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 611, etc.: _plural_, δίκαια γὰρ τόνδ᾽ εὐτυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1126, compare “Tr.” 495, 1116}}; δικαίως ἄν, with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276a. [δικαίων}} with _penultimate_ short in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 247.2}}; compare οὐ δίκαον· οὐ δίκαιον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus]}}

1343:
** δῐκαι-οσύνη**, ἡ,
**righteousness, justice,** {{LXX+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 147, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 433a, LXX.Gen.15.6, etc.}}; δ. δικαστική legal **justice,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a27}}; opposed to ἐπιείκεια, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1137a32.}}
**2** **fulfilment of the Law,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.26.2, +others, NT.Matt.3.15, +others}}
**II** **justice, the business of a judge,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464b, 464c (variant{δικαστική}), “Clit.” 408b.}}]
**III** Δ., personified, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.164; Ἶσις Δ. “SIG” 1131 (from Delos), “IG” 3.203.}}
**IV** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **four,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 23.}}
**V** δικαιοσύνη· ἡ χοῖνιξ, μυστικῶς, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1344:
** δῐκαι-όω**,
_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐδικαίευν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.100:}}_future_ -ώσω Oracle texts cited in [same work]{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.92.β, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; **-ώσομαι** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.40}}:_aorist_ ἐδικαίωσα {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.71}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ωθήσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Sir.18.2}}: _aorist_ ἐδικαιώθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 393 (Lyric poetry)}}: _perfect_ δεδικαίωμαι {{LXX|LXX.Eze.21.13(18).}}
**I** **set right,** νόμος.. δικαιῶν τὸ βιαιότατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 169.3; δικαιωθείς}}**proved, tested,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [prev. cited]}}
**II** **hold** or **deem right, claim** or **demand as a right,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, 133, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31; δεινά με δικαιοῖ δρᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 640, compare 575; δικαιοῦντες μὴ ἀφαιρεθῆναι αὐτήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41: with}}_infinitive_ omitted, οὕτω δ. (i.e. γενέσθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.42}}; δίκας δ. (i.e. γενέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 93; ὅποι ποτὲ θεὸς δικαιοῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 781; οὐκ ὀρθῶς δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; **pronounce judgement,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.71}}: with _infinitive_, ἐδικαίωσεν ἀποδοῦναι ἡμᾶς τὸ κεφάλαιον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 119.14 (1st c.AD)}}; **consent,** δουλεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172, compare 6.86}}; οὐκ ἐδικαίου οὐδένα οἱ ἐσαγγεῖλαι he **would** not **allow**.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.118}}:—_passive_, τὸ δικαιωθὲν ὑπό τινος **that which is ordained,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.1.}}
**III** **do a man right** or **justice:** hence,
**III.1** **chastise, punish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.100}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 934b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 57.47}}; **pass sentence on,** ὑμᾶς αὐτοὺς δικαιώσεσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}
**III.2** _passive_, also, **have right done one,** opposed to ἀδικεῖσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1136a18.}}
**III.3** **pronounce and treat as righteous, justify, vindicate,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.23.7, LXX.Jer.3.11; ἑαυτούς NT.Luke.16.15, etc.:—frequently in}}_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.35, etc.}}

1345:
** δῐκαί-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**act of right,** opposed to ἀδίκημα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359a25}}; **duty,** τὰ πρὸς ἀνθρώπους δ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.199}}; properly **amendment of a wrong,** opposed to δικαιοπράγημα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1135a13: hence}}
**a** **judgement, penalty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864e.}}
**b** **justification, plea of right,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.25, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 279b9, LXX.2Ki.19.28(29), “PLond.” 2.360.8 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; δικαιώματα Ἑλληνίδων πόλεων, compiled by [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus for Philip, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {Δρύμος}.}}]
**c** _plural_, **pleadings, documents** in a suit, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 13.13 (from Samos), “PLille” 29.25 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; also, **credentials**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 113.10 (2nd c.AD), +others}}
**d** **act of** δικαίὠσις {{NT|1.3, NT.Rom.5.16.}}
**II** **ordinance, decree,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.26.5, LXX.Exo.15.26 (_plural_), +others, NT.Rom.1.32, 2.26 (_plural_), +others}}

1346:
Included with: ** δίκαιος** [ῐ], α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 901 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 1202, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.72}}: (δίκη):
**A** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and all writers, of persons, **observant of custom** or **rule,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.52}}; especially **of social rule, well-ordered, civilized,** ὑβρισταί τε καὶ ἄγριοι οὐδὲ δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.175, compare 8.575; [Γαλακτοφάγοι] δικαιότατοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.6; [Χείρων] δικαιότατος Κενταύρων 11.832, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 314, 794}}; δ. πολίτης a **good** citizen, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.21, etc.}}: _metaphorically_ of the sea, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 12.2 (_superlative_)}}; δικαίη ζόη a **civilized** way of living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.177.}} _adverb_ δικαίως, μνᾶσθαι woo **in due form, decently,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.90; ὑπὸ ζυγῷ λόφον δ. εἶχον}}**loyally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 292.}}
**A.2** **observant of duty** to gods and men, **righteous,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.209, etc.; δ. πρὸς πᾶσαν ὁμιλίην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 2; ἰθὺς καὶ δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; opposed to δυσσεβής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 598, compare 610; δ. καὶ ὅσιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507b; δικαίων ἀδίκους φρένας παρασπᾷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 791 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of actions, etc., **righteous,** ἐπὶ ῥηθέντι δικαίῳ a thing **rightly** said, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.414, etc.}}
**A.3** ὁ δίκαιος, euphemistic of a sacred snake, {{Refs|“GDI” 5056 (from Crete).}}
**B** later:
**B.I** **equal, even, well-balanced,** ἅρμα δίκαιον **evengoing** chariot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.26: so _metaphorically_, νωμᾷ δικαίῳ πηδαλίῳ στρατόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86; δικαιόταται ἀντιρροπαί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7}}; δικαιότατα μοχλεύειν {{Refs|[prev. passage]: hence}}**fair, impartial,** βάσανος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.8; συγγραφεύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 39.}}
**B.I.b** **legally exact, precise,** τῷ δικαιοτάτῳ τῶν λόγων to speak **quite exactly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.108, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44}}; of Numbers, αἱ ἑκατὸν ὀργυιαὶ δίκαιαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}. _adverb_ -αίως, πάντα δ. ὑμῖν τετήρηται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.3; δ. ἐξετάζειν [prev. work]154.}}
**B.I.2** **lawful, just,** especially τὸ δ. **right,** opposed to τὸ ἄδικον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 189 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τὸ δ. τὸ νόμιμον καὶ τὸ ἴσον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129a34; δ. διορθωτικόν, διανεμητικόν, [prev. work] 1131b25, 27}}; τὸ πολιτικὸν δ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1134b18; ἔστι ἐπιεικὲς τὸ παρὰ τὸν γεγραμμένον νόμον δ. [prev. author] “Rh.” 1374a27, compare “EN” 1137b12; καὶ δίκαια κἄδικα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 99; τὰ ἴσα καὶ τὰ δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.67}}; τοὐμὸν δ. my own **right,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 810}}; ἐλθεῖν ἐπὶ τοῦτο τὸ δ. bring the case to this **issue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.24}}; οὐδὲν τῶν δ. ποιεῖν τινί not to do **what is just and right** by a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.10}}; τὰ δ. ἔχειν, λαμβάνειν, receive **one's due,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.7.14, 17}}; τὰ δ. πράττεσθαι πόλιν give a city its **deserts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 812}}; ἐκ τοῦ δικαίου, ={δικαίως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1435, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89}}; so ἀπὸ τοῦ δικαίου, τῶν δικαίων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 50.8 (2nd c.BC), 123.8 (1st c.BC)}}; μετὰ τοῦ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.177}}; τὸ δίκαιον **lawful claim,** ἃ ἔχομεν δίκαια πρός.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.179, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 3, etc.}}; τὰ πρὸς ἀλλήλους δ. mutual **obligations** or **contracts,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.21.10}}; ἐπὶ συγκειμένοις τισὶ δικαίοις on certain agreed **terms,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.51}}. _adverb_ **-αίως** **rightly, justly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137; μεῖζον ἢ δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); καὶ δ. καὶ ἀδίκως 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.135.}}
**B.II** of persons and things, **meet and right, fitting,** δ. τοῦδε τοῦ φόνου ῥαφεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1604; κόσμος οὐ φέρειν δ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 55}}; ἵππον δ. ποιεῖσθαί τινι make a horse **fit for** another's **use,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.5, compare “Cyn.” 7.4 (ἵππος δ. τὴν σιαγόνα}} having a **good** mouth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.196).}}
**B.II.b** **normal,** σχήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 69; φύσις [prev. author] “Fract.” 1 (_superlative_).}}
**B.II.2** **real, genuine,** γόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” [1119]; ποιῶν τὰ ἐν τῇ τέχνῃ δ. “Supp.epigram” 2.184.7 (Tanagra, 2nd c.BC).}} _adverb_, εἴπερ δικαίως ἐστ᾽ ἐμός **really and truly** mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 547, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418e.}}
**B.II.3** ὁ δ. λόγος the plea **of equity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76}}. _adverb_ **-αίως** **with reason,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.34, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 675}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1149, etc.}} also **-οτέρως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.170}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1222}}; _Aeolic dialect_ δικαίτατα {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).526c17 (from Eresus).}}
**B.III** ψυχὴ ἐς τὸ δ. ἔβη 'the land of the leal', {{Refs|“IG” 7.2543.3 (from Thebes).}}
**C** in Prose, δίκαιός εἰμι, with _infinitive_, δίκαιοί ἐστε ἰέναι you **are bound** to come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, compare 8.137; δ. εἰμεν ἔχειν [prev. author] 9.27}}; δ. εἰμι κολάζειν I **have a right** to punish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1434, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 400; δ. ἐστι περιπεσεῖν κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7}}; δ. εἰσι ἀπιστότατοι εἶναι they **have most reason** to distrust, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17; δ. βλάπτεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.12; δ. ἐστιν ἀπολωλέναι}}**dignus est qui pereat,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.37}}; ὁ σπουδαῖος ἄρχειν δ. **has a right** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b12}}; with a non-personal subject, ἔλεος δ. ἀντιδίδοσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}: less frequently in _comparative_ and _superlative_, δικαιότεροι χαρίσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.34; δικαιότατος εἶ ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 172b}}; but δίκαιόν ἐστι is also found, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.39, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 611, etc.: _plural_, δίκαια γὰρ τόνδ᾽ εὐτυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1126, compare “Tr.” 495, 1116}}; δικαίως ἄν, with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276a. [δικαίων}} with _penultimate_ short in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 247.2}}; compare οὐ δίκαον· οὐ δίκαιον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus]}}

1347:
** δῐκαί-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**setting right, doing justice to:** hence,
**1** **condemnation, punishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.43 (_plural_), conjecture in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.421d.}}
**2** **plea of legal right, justification,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.8, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}}
**3** **making** or **accounting righteous, justification,** {{NT|NT.Rom.4.25, etc.}}
**II** **demand of right** or **as of right, just claim,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 18.}}
**III** **judgement of what is right,** ἀντήλλαξαν τῇ δικαιώσει altered **at their will and pleasure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82.}}

1348:
** δῐκαστ-ής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**a judge,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, 3.14, 31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 120, “Eu.” 81, etc.}}
**2** of stars, δ. τῶν ὅλων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.31.}}
**3** at Athens and elsewhere, **juror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1136, etc.}}; opposed to νομοθέτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.4, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.10.}}
**II** δ. αἵματος **avenger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1150.}}

1349:
** δίκη** [ῐ], ἡ,
**custom, usage,** αὕτη δ. ἐστὶ βροτῶν this is the **way** of mortals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.218; ἡ γὰρ δ. ἐστὶ γερόντων 24.255, etc.; ἥ τ᾽ ἐστὶ δ. θείων βασιλήων 4.691; ἡ γὰρ δμώων δ. ἐστίν 14.59, etc.}}; ἡ γὰρ δ., ὁππότε.. this is always **the way,** when.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|19.168 (so in late Prose, ἥπερ ἱππομαχίας δ. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.15.2)}}; δίκαν ἐφέπειν τινός to imitate him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.50}}; δ. ἐπέχειν τινός to be **like**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 6.18}}; **normal course of nature,** ἐκ τουτέων ὁ θάνατος οὐ γίνεται κατά γε δίκην, οὐδ᾽ ἢν γένηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 3: hence}}
**2** _adverbial_ in _accusative_ δίκην, **in the way of, after the manner of,** with _genitive_, λύκοιο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.84; πώλου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659; τοξότου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705e}}; in later Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395b22, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 31, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.6, etc.: mostly of living creatures or persons, but also of things, as δίκην ὕδατος, ἀγγείου, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 85 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235d.}}
**II** **order, right,** μή τι δίκης ἐπιδευές nothing short of **what is fit,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.180; opposed to βία, might, 16.388}}; opposed to σχέτλια ἔργα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.84; personified, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 902, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 662, etc.; Δίκης βωμός [prev. author] “Ag.” 383 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 539 (Lyric poetry)}}; **Truth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.71.}}
**II.2** δίκη ἐστί, ={δίκαιόν ἐστι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 259, compare 811, “Eu.” 277.}}
**II.3** Adverb. usages, δίκῃ **duly, rightly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.542, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 112e; ἐν δίκᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.12, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1069, etc.; σὺν δίκῃ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 197, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.96, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 444, etc.; κατὰ δίκην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.35, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 888, etc.; μετὰ δίκης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 643e; πρὸς δίκης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1014, “El.” 1211 (but πρὸς δίκας}} on the score **of justice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 546 (Lyric poetry)); διαὶ δίκας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 641; ἐκ δίκης 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.77: opposed to παρὰ δίκαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.18, etc.; ἄνευ δίκης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 554; πέρα δίκης [prev. author] “Pr.” 30; βίᾳ δίκης [prev. author] “Supp.” 430 (Lyric poetry)}}; δίχα δίκης without **trial,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 32}}; πρὸ δίκης in preference to **legal proceedings,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141.}}
**III** **judgement,** δίκην ἰθύντατα εἰπεῖν give **judgement** most righteously (compare ἰθύς), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.508: especially in _plural_, Λυκίην εἴρυτο δίκῃσί τε καὶ σθένεϊ ᾧ 16.542; περὶ οἶδε δίκας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.244, etc.; δίκαι σκολιαί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 219, 250; κρῖνε εὐθεῖαν δίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 433.}}
**IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of **proceedings instituted to determine legal rights,** hence,
**IV.1** **lawsuit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.210, etc.}}; properly **private suit** or **action,** opposed to γραφή (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.44, etc.; ἐκαλοῦντο αἱ γραφαὶ δίκαι, οὐ μέντοι αἱ δίκαι καὶ γραφαί 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.41}}; οἱ δίκην ἔχοντες the parties to **a suit,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.21.8 (from Megara), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 17.}}
**IV.2** **trial** of the case, πρὸ δίκης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.10, etc.; μέχρι τοῦ δίκην γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53}}; **court** by which it was tried, ἐν ὑμῖν ἐστι καὶ τῇ δίκῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.6.}}
**IV.2.b** δίκην εἰπεῖν to plead a **cause,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.1; δ. μακρὰν λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 776, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 12.}}
**IV.3** **the object** or **consequence of the action, atonement, satisfaction, penalty,** δίκην ἐκτίνειν, τίνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.94, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 113}}: adverbially in _accusative_, τοῦ δίκην πάσχεις τάδ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 614}}; frequently δίκην or δίκας διδόναι suffer **punishment,** i. e. make **amends** (but δίκας δ., in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 703 (Lyric poetry)}}, to grant **arbitration**); δίκας διδόναι τινί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, compare 5.106; ἔμελλε τῶνδέ μοι δώσειν δίκην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 538, etc.}}; also ἀντί or ὑπέρ τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 433, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42}}; also δίκην διδόναι ὑπὸ θεῶν **to be punished** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525b}}; but δίκας ἤθελον δοῦναι they consented to submit to **trial,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28}}; δίκας λαμβάνειν sometimes ={δ. διδόναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115; δίκην ἀξίαν ἐλάμβανες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1312, “Heracl.” 852}}; more frequently its correlative, inflict **punishment,** take **vengeance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.29, etc.; λαβεῖν δίκην παρά τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.92, compare 9.2, etc.}}; so δίκην ἔχειν to have **one's punishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529c (but ἔχω τὴν δ.}} have **satisfaction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 319e; παρά τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45)}}; δίκας or δίκην ὑπέχειν stand **trial,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.118, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 552; δίκην παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 50}}; θανάτου δίκην ὀφλεῖν ὑπό τινος to incur the death **penalty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39b; δίκας λαγχάνειν τινί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.78; δίκης τυχεῖν παρά τινος [prev. work]142; δίκην ὀφείλειν, ὀφλεῖν, [prev. author] 21.77, 47.63; ἐρήμην ὀφλεῖν τὴν δ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13}}; δίκην φεύγειν try to escape **it,** be the defendant in **the trial** (opposed to διώκειν prosecute), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.2}}; δίκας αἰτέειν demand **satisfaction,** τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.114; δ. ἐπιτιθέναι τινί [prev. author] 1.120}}; τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.5; δίκαι ἐπιφερόμεναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302b24; δίκας ἀφιέναι τινί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.79}}; δίκας ἑλεῖν, see at {ἔρημος} 11; δίκην τείσασθαι, see at {τίνω} 11; δὸς δὲ δίκην καὶ δέξο παρὰ Ζηνί {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 312}}; δίκας διδόναι καὶ λαμβάνειν παρ᾽ ἀλλήλων, of communities, submit **causes** to trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.83; δίκην δοῦναι καὶ λαβεῖν ἐν τῷ δήμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.18, etc.}}; δίκας δοῦναι καὶ δέξασθαι submit differences to a peaceful settlement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.59.}}
**V** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **three,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.381f, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 12}}; for **five,** {{Refs|[prev. work]31. (Cf. Sanskrit}}**diś-, diśā** 'direction', 'quarter of the heavens'.)

1350:
** δίκτῠον**, τό,
(δικεῖν) **net:**
**1** **fishing-net,** δικτύῳ ἐξέρυσαν πολυωπῷ (i.e. ἰχθύας) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.386; φελλοὶ δ᾽ ὣς ἄγουσι δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 506; μολυβδὶς ὥστε δ. κατέσπασεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 840}}; δ. καθιέναι, ἀναιρεῖσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b19, 602b8.}}
**2** **hunting-net,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1083, etc.}}; larger than ἄρκυς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.5, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.26, 27.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, δ. ἄτης, Ἅιδου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1078 (_anapaest meter_), “Ag.” 1115 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 932.}}
**4** **lattice-work,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).165.4, 13 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**5** **bottom of a sieve,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1357:
** διόρθ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**making straight,** as in the setting of a limb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 16, compare “Mochl.” 38}}; **setting straight, restoration,** οἰκοδομημάτων καὶ ὁδῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b21.}}
**2** **correction, chastisement,** ἐπὶ διορθώσει {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.56.14; διορθώσεως σφίσι δεῖν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.20.}}
**II** generally, **amendment, correction,** of men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.11.2: _plural_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1317a35, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.118.12; τῶν νόμων “IG” 9(1).694.137 (Corc.)}}; **correction,** ἐρωτημάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 176b34, compare “Pol.” 1275a20; εἰς δ. ἄγειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.58.4}}; δ., opposed to βλάβη, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] 5.88.2; ὑδάτων 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.4 at Title Page}}
**II.2** **right treatment,** τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 642a.}}
**III** **recension, revised edition of** a work, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.397}}: in _plural_, **emendations,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.66.}}
**IV** **payment,** ὀψωνίων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.50.7, compare “PTeb.” 61 (a). 33 (2nd c.BC).}}

1358:
** διορ-ύσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **διορύττω**, **dig through,** διὰ τάφρον ὀρύξας **having dug** a trench **across** or **along,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.120}}; τοῖχον δ., ={τοιχωρυχέω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 565, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.37; δεσμωτήριον [prev. author] 25.56; οἰκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.30, “PPetr.” 3p.60: with _accusative_ loci, τὸν Ἄθω 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.29, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 699a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.30}}: —_passive_, {{NT|NT.Matt.24.43.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **undermine, ruin,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.30; φιλίαν 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 1.123; δημοκρατίαν [prev. author] “Decl.” 1.41}}:—_passive_, διορωρυγμένα δωροδοκίαις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 12, compare 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 5.6}} (but **to be entrenched** in our **several** cities, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.28).}}
**II** **worm out,** ἀπόρρητα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 6; τὰ βουλευόμενα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.87c.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be shut up in a funeral vault,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.43.}}

1359:
** Δῐόσ-κοροι**
(the _Attic dialect_ form, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 212), _Ionic dialect_ and later **Διόσκουροι**, οἱ, **the sons of Zeus,** i. e. **the twins of Leda,** Castor and Polydeuces, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 33.1, etc.: dual Διοσκόρω, τώ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 285, “Ec.” 1069, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 465, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 846, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.253d: _sing._ uncertain in 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 120, cf. Varro “LL” 5.66: Διόσκοροι is required by metre in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1643, “El.” 1239}}: **-κούρων** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 293a}}; **-κόρων** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 796b, codex Laur. in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75: both forms in codices of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.43, 6.127.}}
**II** constellation named from them **the Twins,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Καταστερισμοί” 10.}}
**III** ={παρωτίδες}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.440, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 410.17.}}

1360:
** διότι**,
_conjunction_ for διὰ τοῦτο ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 242.1)}}, **because, for the reason that, since,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44, 3.55, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.52, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.4}}; οὐδὲ δι᾽ ἓν ἄλλο ἢ διότι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100c; answering to διὰ τ; [prev. author] “Plt.” 310d, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 14.6, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 2.4 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.}}
**2** indirect, **wherefore, for what reason,** φράσω διότι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24}}; σκοπεῖν διότι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; ἐρωτᾶν διότι.. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 4.7.}}
**II** ={ὅτι}, **that,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.43, 50, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.48, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.18, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.50, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1062a6, +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.84 W.; τὸ δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 53b9}}: followed by _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.12.4, see reading in 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.76.}}

1362:
** διπλόος**, η, ον,
_contraction_ **διπλοῦς**, ῆ, οῦν, _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ διπλέη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42}} codices, but διπλήν or **-ῆν** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.90}}, διπλάς or **-ᾶς** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.28}}: _contraction_ always in Trag., except διπλόοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 39: (compare ἁπλόος)}}:—**twofold, double,** properly of cloaks and articles of dress, χλαῖνα διπλῆ, ={δίπλαξ} or διπλοΐς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.134, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.226}}; ὅθι.. διπλόος ἤντετο θώρηξ where the cuirass met [the buckle] **so as to be double,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.133}}; τὴν ἐπωμίδα πτύξας διπλῆν having folded it **double,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 4: generally, καλύβη διπλῆ διαφράγματι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.133; διπλόος θάνατος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.104}}; παῖσον διπλῆν (i.e. πληγήν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1415; δ. οἰκίδιον}}**of two stories,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.9}}; διπλῆ ἄκανθα spine **bent double** by age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 492}}; διπλῆ [ῥάχις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.11; σύμβολον δ.}}**executed in duplicate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.29 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** διπλῇ χερὶ θανεῖν by **mutual** slaughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 14.}}
**3** δ. ὀνόματα **compound** words, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1459a9, “Rh.” 1404b29, etc.}}
**4** of fevers in which two paroxysms took place in a given time, δ. ἀμφημερινός, τριταῖος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.472, 9.677.}}
**5** δ. ἰσότης, ={διπλοϊσότης} (which see), Dioph.p.98T., etc.
**6** δ. ἄνδρας· τὰ δισύλλαβα ἀνδρῶν ὀνόματα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** as _comparative_, **twice as much, large,** etc., βίος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75b; δίκη [prev. author] “Lg.” 865c}}; δ. ἢ.. **twice as much as**.. (see. διπλῇ): with _genitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 35b}}; διπλοῦν ὀφείλειν ὅσον.. Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.28}}; διπλῷ, ={διπλῇ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 722b.}}
**III** _plural_, in Trag., ={δύο}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 950, “Ch.” 761, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 960, “OT” 20, “Ant.” 51.}}
**IV** **double, doubtful,** οὐ γνώμᾳ διπλόαν θέτο βουλάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.89; διπλᾶς καὶ ἀμφιβόλους λέξεις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.302.}}
**IV.2** **double-minded, treacherous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 395, etc.; οὐδὲν δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.32; δ. καὶ ποικίλος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 11.5}}; also, **playing two parts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397e}}; **at variance with oneself,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 554d.}}
**V** διπλοῦν, τό, ={δίπλωμα}{{Refs 1st c.AD+|111, 1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.29, +others}}

1363:
** διπλ-όω**,
**repeat** a process, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 91a21}}; **double,** τρίβωνα, of philosophers, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.22}}; **multiply by two,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 159.27}}:—_passive_, ἐδεδίπλωτο ἡ φάλαγξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.19}}; of swords, **to be bent double,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 41}}; of a bow-string, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 3.8}}; of fevers (compare διπλοῦς), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.472}}; δεδιπλωμένον ἔμβρυον, of position of foetus at birth, Aspasia cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.22, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.55.}}
**II** **repay twofold,** δ. διπλᾶ κατὰ τὰ ἔργα αὐτῆς {{NT|NT.Rev.18.6.}}

1366:
** δί-στομος**, ον,
(στόμα) **double-mouthed, with two entrances,** πέτρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 16}}; δ. ὁδοί **double-branching** roads, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 900}}; so of rivers **with two mouths,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 34.10.5}}; **with two harbours,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** of a weapon, **two-edged,** ξίφος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 983; πελέκεως γένυς [prev. author] “fragment” 530.5.}}

1368:
** διῡλ-ίζω**,
**strain, filter thoroughly,** οἶνον Mim. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Oxy.” 413.154, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.72, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.48}}:—_passive_, διυλισμένος οἶνος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Amos.6.6: _metaphorically_, διυλισμένα ἀρετὰ ἀπὸ παντὸς τῶ θνατῶ πάθεος 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.108, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69a.}}
**II** **strain off,** κώνωπα {{NT|NT.Matt.23.24.}}

1369:
** δῐχάζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.10}}:—**divide in two,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 264d (of logical dichotomy)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 3.33, +others}}; αἴγειρος.. δισσοῖσι κλάδοις δεδιχασμένη ἑνὸς ἐκ στελέχους Lyric poetry in{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|“Philol.” 80.334; δεδιχασμένον διχασμῷ 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Deu.14.6.}}
**b** **divide by two,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.7, +others (_passive_).}}
**2** δ. τινὰ κατά τινος **divide** one against another, {{NT|NT.Matt.10.35.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be divided,** interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.18}}; διχαζούσης ἡμέρας at **mid**-day, {{Refs|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}

1371:
** δῐχο-τομέω**,
**cut in twain: bisect** a line, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.28.2}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 913b31; σώματος -ηθέντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 24}}: _metaphorically_ of the medial raphe of the perineum, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.62, etc.}}
**2** **punish with the last severity,** {{NT|NT.Matt.24.51.}}
**3** **divide into two** (logically), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 642b22, 644b19.}}
**4** _intransitive_, of the moon, uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.929f.}}

1372:
** διψ-άω**,
late _Epic dialect_ **διψ-ώω** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 548, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.57 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}: _Ionic dialect_ **διψ-έω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 68}}; _participle_ διψεῦσα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.21}}; _contraction_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ διψῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35b}}; _infinitive_ διψῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 735, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 441, etc.}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐδίψη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.1.β,γ (διψᾷς, -ᾷ, -ᾶν}} only in later writers, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|“APl.” 4.137 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366a, LXX.Isa.29.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.837)}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.17}}: _aorist_ ἐδίψησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562c}}: _perfect_ δεδίψηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 73:}} —_middle_ (see. below):—**thirst,** στεῦτο δὲ διψάων [ᾱ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.584, etc.}}; of the ground, **to be thirsty, parched,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24; δ. ὑπὸ καύματος 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39.2}}; of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.22.5}}:—_middle_, διψώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 25.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, δ. τινός **thirst after** a thing, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.6; ἐλευθερίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562c: later with _accusative_, δ. Χῖον 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.8 H.; φόνον “APl.” 4.137 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus); δικαιοσύνην NT.Matt.5.6; αἷμα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.32.2; also δ. πρὸς τὸν θεόν LXX.Psa.41(42).2: with _dative_, ὕδατιLXX.Exo.17.3}}: with _infinitive_, διψῶ χαρίζεσθαι ὑμῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.1; ἀκρατῶς ἐδίψη οἴνου πίνειν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.41, etc.}}

1375:
** διωγ-μός**, ὁ,
**the chase,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.21, etc.}}
**2** **pursuit,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.13, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 34.4, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 31.191.}}
**II** **persecution, harassing,** in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 148, 1046, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 412}}; also in later Prose, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.483a: _sing._, NT.Matt.13.21, NT.Act.8.1.}}

1377:
** διώκω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ διωκέμεναι, -έμεν (see. below): _future_ **-ξω**{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.21, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.13 (assuming variant), “An.” 1.4.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.16 codices; but διώξομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 368 (and Elmsl. restored διώξει for -εις in “Eq.” 969, “Nu.” 1296, “Th.” 1224), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168a:}}_aorist_ ἐδίωξα: _aorist 2_ ἐδιώκαθον (see. διωκάθω): _perfect_ δεδίωχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 16:}} —_middle_ (see. below):—_passive_, _future_ διωχθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.95, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 2.13}}; but διώξομαι in _passive_ sense, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Amos.2.16, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.20}}: _aorist_ ἐδιώχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.73, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.3, 6, (ἐπ-, κατ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.69, 3.4}}: _perfect_ δεδίωγμαι {{NT|NT.Matt.5.10: (compare ϝιώκω “GDI” 3153 (Corinthian vase)}}; see at {δίω}):—**cause to run, set in quick motion,** opposed to φεύγω:
**1** **pursue, chase,** in war or hunting, φεύγοντα διώκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.199, etc.: _absolutely_, πεδίοιο διωκέμεν ἠδὲ φέβεσθαι 5.223, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.11:}} —_middle_, διώκεσθαί τινα πεδίοιο, δόμοιο, **chase** one over or across.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.602, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.8.}}
**1.b** with _accusative_ _person_, of a lover, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica}} [same place]; **follow,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.13; τοὺς εὐγνώμονας [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.8.6; δ. καὶ φιλεῖν τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168a, cf. NT.Luke.17.23.}}
**2** **pursue** an object, **seek after,** ἀκίχητα διώκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.75; σὸν μόρον δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 997; τιμὰς δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63}}; ἡδονήν, τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ καλόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251a, “Grg.” 480c}}; ἀλήθειαν{{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 482e; δικαιοσύνην NT.Rom.9.30; λαθραίαν Κύπριν 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 67.9: proverbial, τὰ πετόμενα δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009b38; κατὰ σκοπὸν δ. NT.Phil.3.14}}; of plants, δ. τοὺς ξηροὺς τόπους **seek** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.4.2}}; δ. τὰ συμβάντα or τὸ συμβαῖνον **follow** or **wait for** the event, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.39, 10.21}}:—_middle_, διώκεσθαι τὸ πλέον ἔχειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.87 (assuming variant)}}; μοῖρα διωξαμένη [αὐτούς] {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1355 (from Messenia).}}
**3** **pursue** an argument, τὴν ἐναντίωσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 454a}}; also, **describe,** ὕμνῳ ἀρετάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).21; τὴν Ἡρακλέους παίδευσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.34}}; **recite,** λόγον {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.958, compare 335 (_passive_).}}
**II** **drive** or **chase away,** διώκω οὔτιν᾽ ἔγωγε I don't **force** any one **away,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.409; ἐκ γῆς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.77}}; **banish,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.έ: _metaphorically_, διώκεις μ᾽ ᾗ μάλιστ᾽ ἐγὼ σφάλην you **push** or **press** me.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 156.}}
**III** of the wind, **drive** a ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.332}}; of rowers, **impel, speed on her way,** ῥίμφα διώκοντες (i.e. τὴν νῆα){{Refs|12.182; νηῦς ῥίμφα διωκομένη 13.162}}; Συριηγενὲς ἅρμα διώκων **driving** it, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.140, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 84; ἄτρυτον δ. πόδα [prev. author] “Eu.” 403, compare “Th.” 371.}}
**III.2** seemingly _intransitive_, **drive, drive on,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.344, 424}}; **gallop, run,** etc., uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 91 (Lyric poetry); ἀναπηδήσαντες ἐδίωκον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.20; ἅμα διώκοντος}}**on the march,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 17: with _accusative_ spatii, διώξας περὶ ὀκτακοσίους σταδίους 4th c.BC: Chares Historicus 17.}}
**III.3** **urge, impel,** βέλος χερί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).35; φόρμιγγα πλάκτρῳ [prev. author] “N.” 5.24}}; especially of music, δ. μοῦσαν Pratin.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Lyric poetry 5; δ. μέλος 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 29}}:—_passive_, ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς διώκομαι.. σὺν τάχει μολεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 871.}}
**III.4** of work, **urge on, carry forward,** σκαφήτρους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 112.2 (1st c.AD).}}
**III.5** _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ δεδιωγμένος **hurried, rapid,** σφυγμοί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.8.}}
**IV** as law-term, **prosecute,** ὁ διώκων **the prosecutor,** opposed to ὁ φεύγων, the defendant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82 (_plural_), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 583, etc.}}; ὁ διώκων τοῦ ψηφίσματος τὸ λέγειν.., **he who impeaches** the clause in the decree.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.59; γραφὰς δ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5; γραφὴν δ. τινά}}**indict,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.69; δ. εἰσαγγελίαν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 9; δ. τινὰ περὶ θανάτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.6}}: with _genitive_ criminis, **accuse of.., prosecute for**.., δ. τινὰ τυραννίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.104; δειλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 368; παρανόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.22, compare διωκάθει; ψευδομαρτυρίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.13, etc.; δ. ἀπάτης εἵνεκεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.136}}; φόνον τινὸς δ. **avenge** another's murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1534 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269a2}}; δίκην δ. **pursue** one's rights at law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.41; δίκας μὴ οὔσας δ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.2}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **accuse** one of doing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.50}}:—_passive_, ὁ διωκόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5; θανάτου ὑπό τινος -εσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 21; with play on 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 698f}}
**V** **persecute,** {{NT|NT.John.5.16, +others; δεδιωγμένοι ἕνεκα δικαιοσύνης NT.Matt.5.10.}}

1378:
** δόγμα**, ατος, τό,
(δοκέω) **that which seems to one, opinion** or **belief,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 538c; δ. πόλεως κοινόν [prev. author] “Lg.” 644d, etc.}}; especially of philosophical **doctrines,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.7, 15.28, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.59, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.204, etc.}}; **notion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d, +others}}
**2** **decision, judgement,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 926d (_plural_)}}; **public decree, ordinance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.6; τὰ τῶν Ἀμφικτυόνων δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.19, compare 18.154}}; δόγμα ποιήσασθαι, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.5}}; especially of Roman **Senatus-consulta,** δ. συγκλήτου {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.13.2, “IG” 12(3).173.22; δ. τῆς βουλῆς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.87.}}

1379:
** δογμᾰτ-ίζω**,
**lay down as an opinion,** ἀϊδίους εἶναι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 19; τὰ αἰσχρά 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.7.18: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.13, +others}}; οἱ -οντες, ={οἱ δογματικοί}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).270}}:—_passive_, τὰ -όμενα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.18.}}
**2** **decree by ordinance,** with _infinitive_, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.83, LXX.1Esd.6.34}}; of the Roman Senate, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.22}}; δ. τινὰ καλήν **declare** her beautiful, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.576 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, τὰ δογματισθέντα {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).173.53 (from Astypalaea), compare [prev. work] 14.759.13 (from Naples).}}
**3** in _passive_, of persons, **submit to ordinances,** {{NT|NT.Col.2.20.}}

1380:
** δοκέω**
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.192, _Attic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἐδόκουν, _Epic dialect_ δοκέεσκον {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.298 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}: —_middle_, δοκέοντο {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.296}}: _participle_ δοκεύμενος{{Refs|[prev. work] 109}}: the _future_ and other tenses are twofold:
**1** _future_ δόξω and _aorist 1_ ἔδοξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.37, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 208, etc.:}}_perfect_ δέδοχα inferred from _pluperfect_ ἐδεδόχεσαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 44.26}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐδόχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.10.8, etc., (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2}}: _perfect_ δέδογμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐδέδοκτο {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.74.}}
**2** regular forms (chiefly Trag., Comedy texts, and late Prose), _future_ δοκήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 388, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 562, etc. (once in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 4.74)}}; _Doric dialect_ δοκησῶ or **-ᾱσῶ**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.150:}}_aorist_ ἐδόκησα, _Epic dialect_ δόκ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.415, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.56, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1041, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1485, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδόκηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 309 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _passive_, _aorist_ ἐδοκήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1417 (_anapaest meter_)}}: _perfect_ δεδόκημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.19, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 763 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 726, also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16}}.; but δεδοκημένος (which see) belongs to δέχομαι.
**I** **expect** (Iterat. of δέκομαι,{{Refs|compare δέχομαι 11.3)}}: hence, **think, suppose, imagine,** (opposed to φρονέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 942 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 146.4):}}
**I.1** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, δοκέω νικησέμεν Ἕκτορα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.192; οὔ σε δοκέω πείθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, compare 11, 27, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.5, etc.: rarely with}}_infinitive_ omitted, δοκῶ.. οὐδὲν ῥῆμα.. κακὸν [εἶναι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 61}}; τούτους τι δοκεῖτε [εἶναι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.26}}; frequently in relating a dream or vision, τεκεῖν δράκοντ᾽ ἔδοξεν she **thought** a serpent produced young, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 527}}; ἐδόκουν αἰετὸν.. φέρειν **methought** an eagle was carrying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 15; ὁρᾷς γὰρ οὐδὲν ὧν δοκεῖς σάφ᾽ εἰδέναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 259: with}}_infinitive_ only, ἔδοξ᾽ ἰδεῖν **methought** I saw,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 408; ἔδοξ᾽ ἀκοῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315e; ἔδοξ᾽ ἐν ὕπνῳ.. οἰκεῖν ἐν Ἄργει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 44}} (sometimes also, as in _signification_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, ἐδοξάτην μοι δύο γυναῖκε.. μολεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 181; ἐν τῷ σταδίῳ.. μέ τις ἐδόκει στεφανοῦν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 272.4).}}
**I.1.b** **think** to do, **purpose,** ὅταν δ᾽ ἀείδειν.. δοκῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 16.}}
**I.2** _absolutely_, **have** or **form an opinion,** περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.65}}; mostly in parenthetic phrases, ὡς δοκῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 264e}}; δοκῶ alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 47, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 126b}}; πῶς δοκεῖ; to call attention to something remarked, τοῦτον, πῶς δοκεῖ; καθύβρισεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 446, compare “Hec.” 1160, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 96, etc.; πόσον δοκεῖ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 399.}}
**I.3** δοκῶ μοι I **seem to myself, methinks,** with _infinitive_, ἐγώ μοι δοκέω κατανοέειν τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, etc.; ἡδέως ἄν μοι δοκῶ κοινωνῆσαί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.25, compare “Oec.” 6.11}}; οὔ μοι δοκῶ I **think** not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e}}; δοκῶ μοι parenthetic, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Thg.” 121d.}}
**I.3.b** δοκῶ μοι I **am determined, resolved,** with _infinitive_ _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 177, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.53, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ _aorist_, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 671, etc.}}: rarely without μοι, **think fit,** σὺ δ᾽ αὐτὸς ἤδη γνῶθι τίνα πέμπειν δοκεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 650.}}
**I.4** **seem, pretend,** with _infinitive_ (with or without _negative_), ὁρέων μὲν οὐδέν, δοκέων δὲ [ὁρᾶν] uncertain reading in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 87; οὔτε ἔδοξε μαθέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10; οὐδὲ γιγνώσκειν δοκῶν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 163; τὰ μὲν ποιεῖν, τὰ δὲ δοκεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314a39; ἤκουσά του λέγοντος, οὐ δοκῶν κλύειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 67; πόσους δοκεῖς.. ὁρῶντας.. μὴ δοκεῖν ὁρᾶ; [prev. author] “Hipp.” 462, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1146, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.6.}}
**I.5** _passive_, **to be considered,** δοκεῖσθαι οὕτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 612d}}; τὰ νῦν δοκούμενα περί τινος the **current opinions,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 490a.}}
**I.6** _middle_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.296; δοκεύμενος.. ἀλύξειν [prev. work] 109.}}
**II** of an Object, **seem,** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_ _present_, δοκέεις δέ μοι οὐκ ἀπινύσσειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.342}}; δόκησε δ᾽ ἄρα σφίσι θυμὸς ὣς ἔμεν ὡς εἰ.. their heart **seemed** just as if.., **felt** as though.., {{Refs|10.415: with}}_infinitive_ _future_, **seem likely,** δοκέει δέ μοι ὧδε λώϊον ἔσσεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.338}}: with _infinitive_ _aorist_ (never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), τί δ᾽ ἂν δοκεῖ σοι Πρίαμος (i.e. ποιῆσαι); 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 935}}; **seem** or **be thought** to have done, especially of suspected persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21}}; **to be convicted,** ἂν ἁλῷ καὶ δοκῇ τοὔργον εἰργάσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.71.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **seem,** as opposed to to reality, τὸ δοκεῖν καὶ τὰν ἀλάθειαν βιᾶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 76; οὐ δοκεῖν ἄριστος ἀλλ᾽ εἶναι θέλει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 592, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527b; in full, τὸ δοκεῖν εἶναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 788 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3** **seem good, be resolved on,** εἰ δοκεῖ σοι ταῦτα{{Refs|[prev. work] 944; τοιαῦτ᾽ ἔδοξε τῷδε Καδμείων τέλει [prev. author] “Th.” 1030.}}
**II.4** frequently _impersonal_, δοκεῖ μοι **it seems** to me, **methinks,** ὥς μοι δοκεῖ εἶναι ἄριστα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.215}}; ὡς ἐμοὶ δοκεῖ as I **think,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 369, etc.}}; τὸ σοὶ δοκοῦν your **opinion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487d: frequently in}}_infinitive_ in parenthesis clause, ὡς ἐμοὶ δοκέειν **to** my **thinking,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.113; δοκέειν ἐμοί [prev. author] 1.172; ἀλλ᾽, ἐμοὶ δοκεῖν, τάχ᾽ εἴσει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 246, etc.}}; without μοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.1.}}
**II.4.b** **it seems good** to me, **it is** my **pleasure,** δοκεῖ ἡμῖν χρῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1350}}: frequently of a public resolution, τοῖσι Ἕλλησι δόξαι.. ἀπαιτέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.3, etc.; ἔδοξεν Ἀργείοισιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 605, compare “Th.” 1010}}; especially in decrees and the like, ἔδοξε τῇ βουλῇ, τῷ δήμῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 372, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, compare “IG” 1.32, etc.; τὰ δόξαντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 29, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.14; παρὰ τὸ δοκοῦν ἡμῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84, etc.}}:—_passive_, δέδοκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.68; οὕτω δέδοκτα; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1277, etc.; εἰ ἐπαινῆσαι δεδόκηται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.19; δεδόχθω τὸ ἄτοπον τοῦτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799e, etc.; τοῦτ᾽ ἐστ᾽ ἐμοὶ δεδογμένον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1; δεδογμέν᾽ [ἐστί].. τήνδε κατθανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 576, compare “OC” 1431; τὰ δεδογμένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; δεδόχθαι τῇ βουλῇ καὶ τῷ δήμῳ “IG” 22.1.12, etc. with _accusative_ _absolutely_, δόξαν}} **when it was decreed** or **resolved,** δόξαν αὐτοῖς ὥστε διαναυμαχεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.79}}; δόξαν δέ σφι (i.e. λιπέσθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148; δόξαν ἡμῖν ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.13}}; ἰδίᾳ δοκῆσάν σοι τόδ᾽..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 129; also δεδογμένον αὐτοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.125, etc.; but also δόξαντος τούτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.36; δόξαντα ταῦτα καὶ περανθέντα [prev. work] 3.2.19.}}
**II.5** **to be reputed,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.56, “P.” 6.40; ἄξιοι ὑμῖν δοκοῦντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76}}; δοκοῦντες εἶναί τι **men who are held** to be something, **men of repute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472a; τὸ δοκεῖν τινὲς εἶναι.. προσειληφότες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.213; τὸ φρονεῖν ἐδόκει τις εἶναι περιττός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 1; οἱ δοκοῦντες 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28 (uncertain), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 295}}; τὰ δοκοῦντα, opposed to τὰ μηδὲν ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 613; μετ᾽ ἀρετῆς δοκούσης ἐς ἀλλήλους γίγνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10}}; **to be an established, current opinion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b24, +others; τὰ δοκοῦντα [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1088a16, +others:}} — _passive_, οἱ δεδογμένοι ἀνδροφόνοι **those who have been found guilty** of homicide, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.28; also αἱ δοκούμεναι Πέρσαις τέχναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 60. (The two senses of δοκέω are sometimes contrasted, τὰ ἀεὶ δοκοῦντα.. τῷ δοκοῦντι εἶναι ἀληθῆ}} that which **seems** true is true **to him who thinks** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e; τὸ δοκοῦν ἑκάστῳ τοῦτο καὶ εἶναι τῷ δοκοῦντι [prev. work] 162c.)}}

1381:
** δοκῐμάζω**,
(δόκιμος) **assay, test,** πορφύραν καὶ χρυσόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.39; τοὺς οἴνους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1118a28; τὰ νομίσματα [prev. author] “HA” 491a21}}:—_middle_, **prove for oneself, choose,** χώραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.10, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 532.11 (uncertain)}}:— _passive_, ἐπειδὰν τὸ ἔργον.. δοκιμασθῇ {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2266.15 (from Delos).}}
**2** of persons, δ. αὐτούς **put** them **to the test, make trial of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.50; δ. τοὺς μηνυτάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.53; φίλους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.1, compare “PEleph.” 1.8 (4th c.BC), etc.}}; also of Apis-bulls, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38.}}
**II** **approve, sanction,** μετὰ δεδοκιμασμένου [λόγου] μὴ ξυνέπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; ἐψηφίσασθε δοκιμάσαντες τοὺς νόμους, εἶτ᾽ ἀναγράψαι τούτους οἳ ἂν δοκιμασθῶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.82; ἄρρενας ἔρωτας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.11e; ὅπῃ ταύτῃ ἀρετὴ δοκιμάζεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407c: with}}_infinitive_, ἐκπονεῖν ἐδοκίμαζε he **approved of** their working, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.4; ἐπειδή.. ἐδοκιμάσθη ταῦτα καλῶς ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35.}}
**II.2** as a political term,
**II.2.a** **approve after scrutiny as fit** for an office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 45.3}}:—_passive_, **to be approved as fit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.6, etc.; δοκιμασθεὶς ἀρχέτω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765b}}; μου δοκιμαζομένου **when** I **was undergoing a scrutiny,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.111}}; δεδοκιμασμένος [ἰατρός] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 106.24 (2nd c.AD),compare “PGnom.” 201 (2nd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, ὃν ὁ Ἥφαιστος ἐδοκίμασεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.3 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὑπὲρ τοῦ στεφανωθῆναι δοκιμάζομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.266.}}
**II.2.b** **pass as fit to serve,** ἱππεύειν δεδοκιμασμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.20, “IG” 22.1126.15, 1369.}}
**II.2.c** **examine and admit** boys **to the class of** ἔφηβοι or ἔφηβοι **to the rights of manhood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 578 (_passive_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 42.2, etc.; ἕως ἐγὼ ἀνὴρ εἶναι δοκιμασθείην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.5; εἰς ἄνδρας δεδοκιμασμένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.28.}}
**II.2.d** **test** an orator's **right to speak** {{Refs|compare (δοκιμασία 4), “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 310.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, **think fit** to do, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 31, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.7.4, etc.}}: with _negative_, **refuse** to do, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.1.28: _absolutely_, “BGU” 248.19 (1st c.AD), etc.}}

1382:
** δοκῐμ-ή**, ἡ,
**proof, test,** interpolated in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.184.}}
**2** **tried** or **approved character,** {{NT|NT.Phil.2.22, cf. NT.2Cor.2.9.}}

1384:
** δόκῐμ-ος**, ον
(_Doric dialect_ α, ον {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.103)}}, (δέχομαι) **acceptable:** hence,
**1** of persons, **trustworthy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28 (_superlative_), 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 67}}; **approved, esteemed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65, +others; δ. παρά τινι [prev. author] 7.117}}; δοκιμώτατος Ἑλλάδι **most approved** by Hellas, her **noblest** son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 277 (_anapaest meter_)}}: with _infinitive_, **of approved ability** to do.., δόκιμος δ᾽ οὔτις.. εἴργειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 87 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** of things, **excellent,** τὸ ἔαρ -ώτατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.162}}; **notable, considerable,** ποταμός {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.129}}; **approved,** κριθὰ καθαρὰ δ. {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” [same place]}}; δ. ἀργύριον **legal** tender, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.24, compare “PLond.” 3.938.6 (3rd c.AD)}}; ὕμνος **acceptable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.11.}}
**3** _adverb_ **-μως** **really, genuinely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 547 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.7.}}

1385:
** δοκός**, ἡ,
later also ὁ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.1}}: (δέχομαι):— **bearing-beam, main beam,** especially in the roof or floor of a house, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.176, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1496}}; any **balk** or **beam,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.744, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.112}}; **bar** of a gate or door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 201}}: also, in _plural_, **firewood,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 127.5 (3rd c.AD): proverbial, ὁ τὴν δοκὸν φέρων}}**one who has 'swallowed a poker',** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413b28}}; ἐν δοκοῖσι is probably _falsa lectio_ for{ἐνδόκοισι} in [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 66.3, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {ἔνδοκος}.}}]
**II** a kind of **meteor,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 2.96, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Ostentis” 10b, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; compare δοκεύς, δοκίας, δοκίς 11.}}

1389:
** δολόω**,
**beguile, ensnare, take by craft,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 273, 1636; φαρμάκῳ δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.212; ὗς πλέγμασι δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.28}}; δολοῦν τινὰ γάμοις **beguile** by the anticipation of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 898 (_anapaest meter_)}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 2.36, 44}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 494, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1288.}}
**II** **disguise,** μορφήν {{Refs|[prev. work] 129}}; **adulterate** incense, wine, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.81, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 59}}; **alloy,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.48 (_passive_)}}; **dye,** τὰ ἔρια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.169.}}

1391:
** δόξᾰ**, ἡ,
(δοκέω, δέκομαι) **expectation,** οὐδ᾽ ἀπὸ δόξης not otherwise than **one expects,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.324, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.344; in Prose, παρὰ δόξαν ἢ ὡς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, etc.}}; ἐν δόξᾳ θέμενος εὖχος **hoping for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).63}}; δόξαν παρέχειν τινὶ μὴ ποιήσεσθαι.. to make one **expect** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.21}}; δόξαν παρέχεσθαί τινι ὡς.., with _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216d}}; ἀπὸ τῆς δ. πεσέειν, = Latin _spe excidere,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.203.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **notion, opinion, judgement,** whether well grounded or not, βροτῶν δόξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.30, compare 8.51; ψυχῆς εὐτλήμονι δόξῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 28 (_anapaest meter_); ἃ δόξῃ τοπάζω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 235; δόξῃ γοῦν ἐμῇ [prev. author] “Tr.” 718}}; κατά γε τὴν ἐμήν, with or without δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472e, “Phlb.” 41b: opposed to ἐπιστήμη, [prev. author] “Tht.” 187bf, “R.” 506c, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b36; φάσεις καὶ δ. [prev. author] “EN” 1143b13}}; opposed to νόησις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534a}}; ἀληθεῖ δόξῃ δοξασταί capable of being subjects of true **opinion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 202b; δ. ἀληθεῖς ἢ ψευδεῖς [prev. author] “Phlb.” 36c; δόξης ὀρθότης ἀλήθεια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1142b11; δ. ἐμποιεῖν περί τινος [prev. author] “Pol.” 1314b22}}; κύριαι δ. philosophical **maxims,** title of work by Epicurus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.86 W., etc.; αἱ κοιναὶ δ.}}**axioms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 996b28.}}
**II.2** **mere opinion, conjecture,** δόξῃ ἐπίστασθαι, ἡγεῖσθαι, **imagine, suppose** (wrongly), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.132, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105; δόξης ἁμαρτία [prev. author] 1.32}}; δόξαι joined with φαντασίαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 254a29 (but distinguished from φαντασίαι, [prev. author] “de An.” 428a20)}}; κατὰ δόξαν, opposed to κατ᾽ οὐσίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534c}}; ὡς δόξῃ χρώμενοι speaking **by guess,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.8, compare 13.8.}}
**II.3** **fancy, vision,** δ. ἀκόνας λιγυρᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.82; δ. βριζούσης φρενός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 275; οὐκ εἰσὶ δόξαι τῶνδε πημάτων [prev. author] “Ch.” 1053, compare 1051}}; of a dream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 780; δ. ἐνυπνίου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.23}}: _plural_, **hallucinations,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 1.17.}}
**III** **the opinion which others have of one, estimation, repute,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.4 ἀνθρώπων δόξαν ἔχειν ἀγαθήν, compare 34; δ. ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα φέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11.}}
**III.2** mostly, **good repute, honour, glory,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.11, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 373 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.64, etc.; δόξαν φύσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91}}; δόξαν σχεῖν τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 157; ἐπὶ σοφίᾳ δ. εἰληφώς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.2; ἐπὶ καλοκἀγαθίᾳ καὶ σωφροσύνῃ δ. ὁμολογουμένην πεποιημένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.8; δόξαν ἀντὶ τοῦ ζῆν ᾑρημένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15; δόξαν εἶχον ἄμαχοι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241b}}; δ. ἔχειν ὥς εἰσι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.17; δ. καταλιπεῖν [prev. author] 3.24: in _plural_, οἱ ἐν ταῖς μεγίσταις δόξαις ὄντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.51.}}
**III.3** rarely of **ill repute,** [δ.] ἀντὶ καλῆς αἰσχρὰν τῇ πόλει περιάπτειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.10; λαμβάνειν δ. φαύλην [prev. author] “Ep.” 3.5; κληρονομήσειν τὴν ἐπ᾽ ἀσεβείᾳ δ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.22.3.}}
**III.4** **popular repute** or **estimate,** εἰσφέρων οὐκ ἀφ᾽ ὑπαρχούσης οὐσίας.. ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ τῆς δόξης ὧν ὁ πατήρ μοι κατέλιπεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.157.}}
**IV** of external appearance, **glory, splendour,** especially of the **Shechinah,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.16.10, +others; δ. τοῦ φωτός NT.Act.22.11}}: generally, **magnificence,** πλοῦτον καὶ δ. {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.31.16, cf. NT.Matt.4.8, +others}}; especially of **celestial beatitude,** {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.17: _plural_, NT.1Pet.1.11}}; also of **illustrious persons, dignities,** δόξας οὐ τρέμουσιν {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.10; δ. βλασφημεῖν NT.Jude.8.}}

1392:
** δοξ-άζω**,
**think, imagine,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|A.Ag. 673, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1043, etc.}}; with _double_ _accusative_, πῶς ταῦτ᾽ ἀληθῆ.. δοξάσ; how **can I suppose** this to be true? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 844; δ. βελτίους ἑαυτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 48e; τὰ εὔχρηστα τῶν ζῴων θεοὺς ἐδόξασαν 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.11; also _absolutely_, μετ᾽ ἀσφαλείας δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120}}; δοξάζων μὲν οὔ not **expecting** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 545:}} —_passive_, δ. εἶναι **to be supposed** to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 46d, +others}}; ὅση δοξάζεται (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 108c; δ. κακοί [prev. author] “Lg.” 646e; δ. δίκαιος [prev. author] “R.” 588b; τὰ δοξαζόμενα [prev. author] “Plt.” 278b.}}
**2** with _participle_, δοξάσει τις ἀκούων **will suppose that** he hears, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 60 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, δόξας δ. **entertain** opinions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 46d}}; δ. ψευδῆ **hold** false **opinions,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 189c; ψευδῶς δοξαζόμενα Polystr. p.26W.}}
**4** _absolutely_, **form** or **hold an opinion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a, +others; περί τινος [prev. author] “Grg.” 461b; κακῶς δ. [prev. author] “R.” 327c; παρὰ τὰ ὄντα [prev. author] “Phdr.” 262b}}; opposed to γιγνώσκω, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 476d}}; opposed to ἐπίσταμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 89a7; δ. ἄνευ ἐπιστήμης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201c.}}
**5** _passive_, **to be matter of opinion,** ταῦτα δεδοξάσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 35, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 22.}}
**II** **magnify, extol,** ἐπὶ πλέον τι αὑτὸν δ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, cf. LXX.Exo.15.2, +others; τὸν θεόν NT.Rom.1.21, +others}}:—_passive_, **to be distinguished, held in honour, magnified,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 2.24; δεδοξασμένος ἐπ᾽ ἀρετῇ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.10, cf. LXX.Exo.15.1, +others, NT.John.7.39, +others; ἱερὸν δεδοξασμένον ἐξ ἀρχαίων “OGI” 168.56 (2nd c.BC).}}

1394:
** δόσις**, εως, ἡ,
(δίδωμι) **giving,** φαρμάκου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18; χρημάτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61; μισθοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143}}; opposed to αἴτησις, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 14d; δ. χρημάτων καὶ λῆψις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b8; λήψεις καὶ δόσεις 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.12, cf. LXX.Sir.41.19, NT.Phil.4.15.}}
**b** **licence, permission**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 987.33 (Chios, 4th c.BC).}}
**2** ἐμβολῶν δ. **ramming** in naval tactics, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.10.}}
**II** **gift,** καί οἱ δ. ἔσσεται ἐσθλή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.213; δ. ὀλίγη τε φίλη τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.208, 14.58, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 9.93, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1518, etc.; δόσιν κακὰν κακῶν κακοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1041 (Lyric poetry); θεῶν εἰς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c.}}
**II.2** **bequest, legacy:** κατὰ δόσιν by **will** (opposed to κατὰ γένος, as heir-at-law), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.45, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}}
**II.3** **largess,** = Latin _congiarium,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.8.8 (_plural_).}}
**II.4** **contribution** towards the fulfilment of a purpose, Chrysipp.(?){{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.30.}}
**II.5** **payment on account, instalment,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.296, 7.303.35 (Orop.), etc.}}; **payment in kind,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 146.4 (6th c.AD), +others}}
**II.6** **portion,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Gen.47.22, +others, “BGU” 1122.12 (1st c.BC)}}; διελὼν εἰς δόσεις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 13.}}
**II.7** **dose of medicine,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.171}}; δ. τελεία full **dose,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.57.5, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 4.}}
**II.8** τῆς οἰκονομίας πολλὴν ποιεῖσθαι δ. lay great **stress** on an arrangement, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 51; οὐ τοσαύτην ποιούμενοι τῆς ἡδονῆς δ. ὅσην τῆς ἀληθείας [prev. work] 18.}}
**II.9** **destiny, fate** of an individual, ἡ ἀνθρωπίνη δ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.3}}; especially of planetary **influence,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.2, +others}}

1396:
** δουλᾰγωγ-έω**,
**make a slave, treat as such,** uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.24, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.24.76.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of pleasure, etc., δ. τοὺς βίους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 44.6, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 2.7}}; τὸ σῶμα **bring** it **into subjection,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.9.27.}}

1397:
** δουλ-εία**, ἡ,
_Ionic dialect_ **δουλ-ηΐη** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 114, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12}}: also **δουλία** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.75}}:— **slavery, bondage,** [same places], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 253}}; δουλείας γάγγαμον, ζυγά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 360 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 944 (Lyric poetry); δ. καὶ ὑπηρεσία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 602}}; ἡ τῶν κρεισσόνων δ. **imposed by** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8; ἡ ὑπὸ τῶν βαρβάρων δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469c}}; applied to the condition of the subject allies of Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9.}}
**II** collectively, **slaves,** δουλεύοντα δουλείαις ἐμαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 803}}; ἢν.. ἡ δ. ἐπανιστῆται if **the slave-class** rise in rebellion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; ἡ Ἡρακλεωτῶν δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 776d; τὰς.. Εἱλωτείας καὶ Πενεστείας καὶ δουλείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1264a36.}}
**III** **service for hire,** μισθὸν δουλείας {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.5.6.}}

1398:
** δουλ-εύω**,
(δοῦλος) **to be a slave,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.138, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777d, etc.; παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129: with _accusative_ _cognate_, δουλείαν δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183a, +others}}
**2** **serve, be subject,** τό τ᾽ ἄρχειν καὶ τὸ δ. δίχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 927, etc.}}; δ. ζεύγλαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 463; τοῖς ἄρχουσι καὶ τοῖς νόμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698b; ἡδονῇ [prev. author] “Phdr.” 238e, etc.}}; δ. γαστρί, ὕπνῳ, λαγνείᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.8; κεναῖς δόξαις Polystr. p.29 W.}}; τῇ γῇ δ. **make oneself a slave** to one's land, i. e. give up rights that one may keep it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81; so δ. τῇ κτήσει αὐτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494d; δουλεύομεν δόξαισιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 93.8}}; δ. τῷ καιρῷ **accommodate oneself** to the occasion, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.441 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus); θυμῷ 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.17.6.}}
**3** **render a service,** τινά {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 5.1727.11 (6th c.AD).}}

1399:
** δοῦλος**
**A**, Cretan dialect **δῶλος** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.1, +others}}, ὁ:—properly **born bondman** or **slave,** opposed to **one made a slave,** τὰ ἀνδράποδα πάντα καὶ δοῦλα καὶ ἐλεύθερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.28, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 330}}: then, generally, **bondman, slave,** opposed to δεσπότης (which see): not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, who twice has _feminine_ **δούλη**, ἡ, **bondwoman,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.409, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.12, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1326, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e, etc.}}: frequently of Persians and other nations subject to a despot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, etc.}}; οὔ τινος δοῦλοι κέκληνται, of the Greeks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 242: _metaphorically_, χρημάτων δ.}} **slaves to** money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 865; so γνάθου δ. [prev. author] “fragment” 282.5; τῶν αἰεὶ ἀτόπων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; λιχνειῶν, λαγνειῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.22, compare “Mem.” 1.3.11.}}
**A.II** _adjective_ (not in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus)}}, δοῦλος, η, ον, **slavish, servile, subject,** δ. πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 917, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.29; γνώμαισι δούλαις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 53; δ. ἔχειν βίον [prev. work] 302}}; σῶμα δ., opposed to νοῦς ἐλεύθερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 940; τοὺς τρόπους δούλους παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 877}}; δ. θάνατος, ζυγόν, πούς, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1170, “Tr.” 678, 507; δ. καὶ τυραννουμένη πόλις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 577d; δ. ἡδοναί, ={δουλοπρεπεῖς}, [prev. work] 587c, etc.: _comparative_ δουλότερος}}] **more enslaved,** Αἴγυπτον δ. ποιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.7.}}
**A.II.2** τὸ δ., ={οἱ δοῦλοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 983, etc.}}; also, **slavery, a slavish life,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 556 (troch.).}}
**A.II.3** **ancillary,** δ. ἐπιστῆμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 996b11.}}
